WEBVTT

1
00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:39.568
<font color=#00ff00>The Sunset Limited </font>
<font color=#00ff00>720p -- 23.976 fps</font>
Original Air Date on February 12, 2011

2
00:00:39.668 --> 00:00:43.168
== sync by <font color=#00ff00>elderman</font> ==
== for <font color=#00ffff>www.addic7ed.com</font> ==

3
00:01:47.674 --> 00:01:50.876
So what am I supposed
to do with you, Professor?

4
00:01:52.665 --> 00:01:54.799
Why are you supposed
to do anything?

5
00:01:54.859 --> 00:01:57.461
Well, like I said, this
ain't none of my doing.

6
00:01:57.504 --> 00:01:59.139
When I left out of here
going to work this morning

7
00:01:59.163 --> 00:02:01.164
you wasn't no part of my plans,

8
00:02:01.206 --> 00:02:02.707
but here you is.

9
00:02:02.756 --> 00:02:05.224
Doesn't mean anything.
Everything that happened

10
00:02:05.259 --> 00:02:07.760
doesn't mean something else.

11
00:02:07.795 --> 00:02:09.662
What's it mean then?

12
00:02:09.697 --> 00:02:11.764
Doesn't mean anything.
You run into people.

13
00:02:11.799 --> 00:02:13.967
And maybe some of them are
in trouble or whatever,

14
00:02:14.001 --> 00:02:16.169
but it doesn't mean you're
responsible for them.

15
00:02:16.203 --> 00:02:17.737
Mm-hmm.

16
00:02:17.771 --> 00:02:20.473
Anyway, people who are always
looking out for perfect strangers

17
00:02:20.507 --> 00:02:22.742
are very often people who
won't look out for the ones

18
00:02:22.776 --> 00:02:24.844
they're supposed to look
out for, in my opinion.

19
00:02:24.878 --> 00:02:27.880
If you're just doing
what you're supposed to,

20
00:02:27.915 --> 00:02:30.283
you don't get to be a hero.

21
00:02:30.317 --> 00:02:31.517
And that would be me?

22
00:02:31.552 --> 00:02:33.252
I don't know.
Would it?

23
00:02:33.287 --> 00:02:35.054
I can see how there might
be some truth in that,

24
00:02:35.089 --> 00:02:37.857
but in this particular case
I got to say I didn't know

25
00:02:37.891 --> 00:02:40.326
what sort of person I was
supposed to be on the lookout for

26
00:02:40.361 --> 00:02:42.595
or what I was supposed
to do when I found them.

27
00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:45.832
In this particular case
but one thing to go by.

28
00:02:45.866 --> 00:02:47.033
And that was?

29
00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:49.635
That was that
they is standing there

30
00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:51.170
and I have to look
at them and say,

31
00:02:51.205 --> 00:02:53.306
"Now he don't look like my
brother, but there he is,

32
00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:56.042
so maybe you'd better
look again."

33
00:02:56.076 --> 00:02:57.677
And that's what you did?

34
00:02:57.711 --> 00:03:00.013
Well, I got to say

35
00:03:00.047 --> 00:03:01.748
you was kind of hard to ignore.

36
00:03:01.782 --> 00:03:04.017
Your approach
was pretty direct.

37
00:03:04.051 --> 00:03:06.185
I didn't approach you.
I didn't even see you.

38
00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:10.223
What I don't understand is how you
come to get yourself in such a fix.

39
00:03:10.257 --> 00:03:11.557
Yeah.

40
00:03:11.592 --> 00:03:13.893
Are you all right?
Did you sleep last night?

41
00:03:13.927 --> 00:03:15.161
No.

42
00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:17.830
Well,

43
00:03:17.865 --> 00:03:19.799
when did you decide
that today was the day?

44
00:03:19.833 --> 00:03:21.534
Was there something
special about it?

45
00:03:21.568 --> 00:03:23.236
No.

46
00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:25.905
Well, today is my birthday, but I
certainly don't regard that as special.

47
00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:27.774
Well, Happy Birthday,
Professor.

48
00:03:27.808 --> 00:03:28.941
<i>Thank you.</i>

49
00:03:28.976 --> 00:03:30.710
So you seen your
birthday was coming

50
00:03:30.744 --> 00:03:32.979
and that seemed
like a good day?

51
00:03:33.013 --> 00:03:35.048
Who knows?
Maybe birthdays are dangerous,

52
00:03:35.082 --> 00:03:37.016
like Christmas...

53
00:03:37.051 --> 00:03:39.819
Ornaments hanging from the
trees, reeds from the doors

54
00:03:39.853 --> 00:03:42.422
and bodies from the
steampipes all over America.

55
00:03:42.456 --> 00:03:44.657
That don't say much
for Christmas, do it?

56
00:03:44.691 --> 00:03:46.626
Christmas is not
what it used to be.

57
00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:49.629
Now I believe that to be a true statement.
I surely do.

58
00:03:49.663 --> 00:03:52.932
I've got to go.

59
00:03:55.369 --> 00:03:57.170
You always put your
coat on like that?

60
00:03:57.204 --> 00:03:58.938
What's wrong with the way
I put my coat on?

61
00:03:58.972 --> 00:04:00.473
I ain't said nothing
was wrong with it.

62
00:04:00.507 --> 00:04:02.175
I'm just asking if that's
your regular method.

63
00:04:02.209 --> 00:04:04.577
- I don't have a regular method. I just put it on.
- Mm-hmm.

64
00:04:04.611 --> 00:04:07.013
It's what... Effeminate?
What?

65
00:04:07.047 --> 00:04:10.016
I'm just studying
the ways of professors.

66
00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:12.452
Well, I've got to go.

67
00:04:12.486 --> 00:04:14.554
Let me get my coat.

68
00:04:14.588 --> 00:04:16.322
- Get your coat?
- Yeah.

69
00:04:16.356 --> 00:04:18.157
- Where are you going?
- Going with you.

70
00:04:18.192 --> 00:04:19.826
What do you mean?
Going with me where?

71
00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:21.461
Going with you wherever
it is you're going.

72
00:04:21.495 --> 00:04:23.362
- No, you're not.
- Yeah, I am.

73
00:04:23.397 --> 00:04:24.764
- I'm going home.
- All right.

74
00:04:24.798 --> 00:04:26.399
All right?
You're not going home with me.

75
00:04:26.433 --> 00:04:28.901
Sure I am.
Let me get my coat.

76
00:04:30.804 --> 00:04:32.538
- You can't go home with me.
- <i>Why?</i>

77
00:04:32.573 --> 00:04:34.340
- You can't.
- Oh, what,

78
00:04:34.374 --> 00:04:37.343
you can go home with me but
I can't go home with you?

79
00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:39.011
No. I mean,
no, that's not it.

80
00:04:39.046 --> 00:04:40.980
I just need to go home.

81
00:04:41.643 --> 00:04:43.611
- You live in an apartment?
- Yes.

82
00:04:43.645 --> 00:04:45.413
- What, they don't let black folk in there?
- No.

83
00:04:45.447 --> 00:04:47.381
I mean, yes, of course they do.

84
00:04:47.416 --> 00:04:49.951
Look, no more jokes.
I've got to go. I'm very tired.

85
00:04:49.985 --> 00:04:53.054
All right, long as you don't run into
no hassle about getting me in there.

86
00:04:53.088 --> 00:04:55.723
- You're serious.
- Oh, I think you know I'm serious.

87
00:04:55.758 --> 00:04:58.126
- You can't be serious.
- I'm serious as a heart attack.

88
00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:00.261
- Why are you doing this?
- Me? I ain't got no choice.

89
00:05:00.295 --> 00:05:02.330
- Of course you have a choice.
- No, I ain't.

90
00:05:02.364 --> 00:05:03.931
Who appointed you
my guardian angel?

91
00:05:03.966 --> 00:05:05.733
You know who appointed
me your guardian angel.

92
00:05:05.768 --> 00:05:07.869
Now look, I ain't ask for
you to jump into my arms

93
00:05:07.903 --> 00:05:10.171
- down at the subway this morning.
- I didn't jump into your arms.

94
00:05:10.205 --> 00:05:11.339
- You didn't?
- No.

95
00:05:11.373 --> 00:05:13.374
Well, how'd you get there then?

96
00:05:27.156 --> 00:05:29.891
What?
Now we ain't going?

97
00:05:33.128 --> 00:05:37.165
Do you really think
Jesus is in this room?

98
00:05:38.567 --> 00:05:40.001
No.

99
00:05:40.035 --> 00:05:42.970
I don't think
he's in this room.

100
00:05:46.508 --> 00:05:49.710
I know he's in this room.

101
00:05:53.682 --> 00:05:55.616
It's the way you
put it, Professor.

102
00:05:55.651 --> 00:05:58.319
It'd be like me asking you if
you think you got your coat on.

103
00:05:58.353 --> 00:06:00.922
It's not the same thing.
It's a matter of agreement.

104
00:06:00.956 --> 00:06:03.524
If you and I say
that I have my coat on

105
00:06:03.559 --> 00:06:07.128
and Cecil says that I'm naked
and have green skin and a tail,

106
00:06:07.162 --> 00:06:09.330
we might want to think about
where we should put Cecil

107
00:06:09.364 --> 00:06:11.833
so he doesn't hurt himself.

108
00:06:11.867 --> 00:06:13.034
Who's Cecil?

109
00:06:13.068 --> 00:06:15.236
He's not anybody.
He's just a hypothetical.

110
00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:17.972
There's not any Cecil.
He's just a character

111
00:06:18.006 --> 00:06:20.508
I made up
to illustrate a point.

112
00:06:20.542 --> 00:06:21.976
- Made up?
- Yes.

113
00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:23.744
So his view of things
don't count?

114
00:06:23.779 --> 00:06:25.413
No. That's why
I made him up.

115
00:06:25.447 --> 00:06:27.882
I could have changed things around.
I could have made you the one

116
00:06:27.916 --> 00:06:29.350
who didn't think
I was wearing a coat.

117
00:06:29.384 --> 00:06:31.819
And was green and all
that other shit you said?

118
00:06:31.854 --> 00:06:33.454
- Yes.
- But you didn't.

119
00:06:33.488 --> 00:06:35.523
- No.
- You load it all off on Cecil.

120
00:06:35.557 --> 00:06:37.191
Yes.

121
00:06:37.226 --> 00:06:38.793
But Cecil can't defend himself

122
00:06:38.827 --> 00:06:41.195
on account that he ain't in
agreement with everybody else,

123
00:06:41.230 --> 00:06:43.831
so his word don't count,
aside from the fact

124
00:06:43.866 --> 00:06:46.334
that you made him up and
he's green and everything.

125
00:06:46.368 --> 00:06:48.736
He's not the one who's green. I am.
Where is this going?

126
00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:50.271
I'm just trying to
find out about Cecil.

127
00:06:50.305 --> 00:06:52.273
I don't think so.

128
00:06:52.307 --> 00:06:54.275
Can you see Jesus?

129
00:06:54.309 --> 00:06:56.844
No, I can't see him.

130
00:06:56.879 --> 00:06:58.846
- But you talk to him.
- Don't miss a day.

131
00:06:58.881 --> 00:07:02.116
- And he talks to you.
- I have heard him, yes.

132
00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:04.051
Do you hear him,
like, out loud?

133
00:07:04.086 --> 00:07:06.053
No no, not out loud.
I don't hear a voice.

134
00:07:06.088 --> 00:07:08.089
I don't hear my own,
for that matter.

135
00:07:08.123 --> 00:07:09.690
But I have heard him.

136
00:07:09.725 --> 00:07:11.726
But why couldn't Jesus
just be in your head?

137
00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:13.861
He is in my head.

138
00:07:13.896 --> 00:07:17.164
Well, then I don't understand what
it is you're trying to tell me.

139
00:07:17.199 --> 00:07:19.333
I know you don't,
honey. Look,

140
00:07:19.368 --> 00:07:21.335
the first thing
you got to understand is

141
00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:23.371
I ain't got an original
thought in my head.

142
00:07:23.405 --> 00:07:26.741
If it ain't got the lingering
scent of divinity to it,

143
00:07:26.775 --> 00:07:29.477
- I ain't interested in it.
- The lingering scent of divinity.

144
00:07:29.511 --> 00:07:31.112
- Yeah, you like that?
- It's not bad.

145
00:07:31.146 --> 00:07:33.114
Heard it on the radio...
a black preacher.

146
00:07:33.148 --> 00:07:35.816
The point is, is I done
tried it the other way.

147
00:07:35.851 --> 00:07:37.785
I don't mean
no chipping neither.

148
00:07:37.819 --> 00:07:41.455
I mean blindfold, running through
the woods, bit in my teeth...

149
00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:43.391
Lord, didn't I try it!

150
00:07:43.425 --> 00:07:46.260
If you can find somebody gave
it a better shot than me,

151
00:07:46.295 --> 00:07:48.529
I'd like to meet him,
I surely would.

152
00:07:48.563 --> 00:07:49.964
And what you think that got me?

153
00:07:49.998 --> 00:07:51.265
I don't know.
What did it get you?

154
00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:53.167
Life in death
is what it got me.

155
00:07:53.201 --> 00:07:54.635
Life in death.

156
00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:56.570
Too dead to even know
enough to lay down.

157
00:07:56.605 --> 00:07:59.507
I see.

158
00:08:00.876 --> 00:08:02.843
I don't think so.

159
00:08:02.878 --> 00:08:04.445
Let me ask you a question.

160
00:08:04.479 --> 00:08:06.147
All right.

161
00:08:06.181 --> 00:08:07.515
You ever read this book?

162
00:08:07.549 --> 00:08:09.717
I've read parts of it.
I've read in it.

163
00:08:09.751 --> 00:08:11.252
Have you ever read it?

164
00:08:11.286 --> 00:08:13.254
Read the book of job.

165
00:08:13.288 --> 00:08:16.791
Have you ever read it?

166
00:08:16.825 --> 00:08:18.092
No.

167
00:08:18.126 --> 00:08:19.927
- But you is read a lot of books?
- Yes.

168
00:08:19.962 --> 00:08:22.163
- How many, you say?
- I have no idea.

169
00:08:22.197 --> 00:08:24.899
Ballpark.

170
00:08:24.933 --> 00:08:26.667
Two a week,

171
00:08:26.702 --> 00:08:29.904
maybe 100 a year
for close to 40 years.

172
00:08:29.938 --> 00:08:32.673
Two a week...

173
00:08:32.708 --> 00:08:34.976
40 times 100 is 4000.

174
00:08:36.144 --> 00:08:39.146
I'm just messing
with you, Professor.

175
00:08:39.181 --> 00:08:41.115
Tell you what...
Give me a number,

176
00:08:41.149 --> 00:08:43.951
any number you like, and I'll
give you 40 times it back.

177
00:08:43.986 --> 00:08:45.753
- 26.
- 1040.

178
00:08:45.787 --> 00:08:48.022
- 118.
- 4720.

179
00:08:48.056 --> 00:08:50.157
- 4720.
- Yep.

180
00:08:50.192 --> 00:08:52.159
- The answer is the question.
- Say what?

181
00:08:52.194 --> 00:08:54.462
- That's your new number.
- 4720?

182
00:08:54.496 --> 00:08:56.630
- <i>That's a big number, Professor.</i>
- Yes, it is.

183
00:08:56.665 --> 00:08:58.799
- You know the answer?
- No, I don't.

184
00:08:58.834 --> 00:09:00.267
188,800.

185
00:09:03.038 --> 00:09:05.139
Let me see that.

186
00:09:16.985 --> 00:09:18.519
How do you do that?

187
00:09:18.553 --> 00:09:20.321
Numbers are
the black man's friend...

188
00:09:20.355 --> 00:09:22.123
Butter and eggs, crap table.

189
00:09:22.157 --> 00:09:24.892
You quick with numbers, you can
work the mojo on your brother,

190
00:09:24.926 --> 00:09:26.827
confiscate the contents
of his pocketbook.

191
00:09:26.862 --> 00:09:30.331
You get a lot of time to practice
that shit in the jailhouse.

192
00:09:30.365 --> 00:09:31.999
I see.

193
00:09:32.034 --> 00:09:34.835
Let's get back to you and
all these books you read.

194
00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:38.005
You say you done read
4000 books?

195
00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:39.373
Probably, maybe more than that.

196
00:09:39.408 --> 00:09:41.042
But not this book.

197
00:09:41.076 --> 00:09:42.410
Uh, no, not the whole...

198
00:09:42.444 --> 00:09:44.378
- Why is that?
- I don't know.

199
00:09:44.413 --> 00:09:47.314
Well, what would you say is
the best book ever wrote?

200
00:09:47.349 --> 00:09:48.482
I have no idea.

201
00:09:48.517 --> 00:09:50.384
Well, take a shot.

202
00:09:50.419 --> 00:09:52.420
There are a lot of good books.

203
00:09:52.454 --> 00:09:54.388
Pick one.

204
00:09:54.423 --> 00:09:56.390
- Maybe "War and Peace."
- All right.

205
00:09:56.425 --> 00:09:58.359
Do you think that book's
as good as this one?

206
00:09:58.393 --> 00:10:00.494
I don't know.
They're different kinds of books.

207
00:10:00.529 --> 00:10:03.431
This "War and Peace" book...
It's a book somebody made up, right?

208
00:10:03.465 --> 00:10:04.665
Well, yes.

209
00:10:04.699 --> 00:10:06.634
So is that what makes it
different from this here book?

210
00:10:06.668 --> 00:10:08.803
No, in my view
they're both made up.

211
00:10:08.837 --> 00:10:11.005
Ain't neither one of them true?

212
00:10:11.039 --> 00:10:12.740
Not in the historical
sense, no.

213
00:10:12.808 --> 00:10:14.408
Hmm.

214
00:10:14.443 --> 00:10:15.976
Well, what would be
a true book?

215
00:10:16.011 --> 00:10:18.646
I suppose maybe a history book.

216
00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:21.348
Gibbon's "Decline and Fall
of the Roman Empire" might be one.

217
00:10:21.383 --> 00:10:23.350
At least the events
would be actual events.

218
00:10:23.385 --> 00:10:24.852
They would be things
that had happened.

219
00:10:24.886 --> 00:10:26.353
Hmm.

220
00:10:26.388 --> 00:10:29.623
So you think that book is as
good a book as this book?

221
00:10:29.658 --> 00:10:31.559
- The Bible?
- The Bible.

222
00:10:31.593 --> 00:10:34.361
I don't know. Gibbon's is a cornerstone.
It's a major book.

223
00:10:34.396 --> 00:10:36.897
- And a true book. Don't forget that.
- And a true book, yes.

224
00:10:36.932 --> 00:10:38.432
But is it as good a book?

225
00:10:38.467 --> 00:10:41.635
I don't know.

226
00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:43.637
I don't know as you can
make a comparison.

227
00:10:43.672 --> 00:10:45.072
We're talking about
apples and pears.

228
00:10:45.107 --> 00:10:47.174
We ain't talking about no
apples and pears, Professor.

229
00:10:47.209 --> 00:10:48.375
We're talking about books.

230
00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:50.377
Is that "Decline and Fall" book

231
00:10:50.412 --> 00:10:52.379
as good a book
as this here book?

232
00:10:52.414 --> 00:10:54.181
Answer the question.

233
00:10:54.216 --> 00:10:55.549
I'm gonna have to say no.

234
00:10:55.584 --> 00:10:58.752
Used to say right here
on the cover

235
00:10:58.787 --> 00:11:00.721
'fore it got wore down...

236
00:11:00.755 --> 00:11:02.223
"The greatest book
ever written."

237
00:11:02.257 --> 00:11:04.525
- Think that might be true?
- It might.

238
00:11:04.559 --> 00:11:07.695
- You read good books.
- <i>I try to, yes.</i>

239
00:11:07.729 --> 00:11:09.864
But you ain't read
the best book.

240
00:11:09.898 --> 00:11:12.500
- Why is that?
- I've got to go.

241
00:11:12.534 --> 00:11:15.136
You don't need to go, Professor.
Just stay here and visit with me.

242
00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:17.538
You' afraid I'll go back
to the train station.

243
00:11:17.572 --> 00:11:19.673
You might, so just
stay here with me.

244
00:11:19.708 --> 00:11:21.142
What if I promised I wouldn't?

245
00:11:21.176 --> 00:11:22.443
You might anyway.

246
00:11:22.477 --> 00:11:24.411
Don't you need to go to work?

247
00:11:24.446 --> 00:11:25.946
I was on my way to work.

248
00:11:25.981 --> 00:11:27.882
A funny thing happened to
you on the way to work.

249
00:11:27.916 --> 00:11:29.416
Yes, it did.

250
00:11:29.451 --> 00:11:30.651
Will they fire you?

251
00:11:30.685 --> 00:11:32.520
No, they ain't gonna fire me.

252
00:11:32.554 --> 00:11:34.755
- You could call in.
- <i>Ain't got no phone.</i>

253
00:11:34.789 --> 00:11:37.124
Anyhow, they know
if I ain't there by no

254
00:11:37.159 --> 00:11:38.893
I ain't coming.
I ain't a late sort of person.

255
00:11:38.927 --> 00:11:40.861
- Why don't you have a phone?
- <i>Don't need one.</i>

256
00:11:40.896 --> 00:11:42.863
Junkies would steal it anyway.

257
00:11:42.898 --> 00:11:44.532
Get a cheap one.

258
00:11:44.566 --> 00:11:47.067
Don't get too cheap
for a junkie.

259
00:11:47.102 --> 00:11:49.904
Let's get back to you.

260
00:11:49.938 --> 00:11:52.339
Let's stick with you
for a minute.

261
00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:00.648
- Can I ask you something?
- Sure you can.

262
00:12:00.682 --> 00:12:03.551
Where were you standing?
I never saw you.

263
00:12:03.585 --> 00:12:05.586
You mean when you took
your amazing leap?

264
00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:08.389
- Yes.
- I was on the platform.

265
00:12:08.423 --> 00:12:09.823
- On the platform?
- Yeah.

266
00:12:09.858 --> 00:12:11.325
Well, I didn't see you.

267
00:12:11.359 --> 00:12:13.861
I was standing on the platform,
minding my own business.

268
00:12:13.895 --> 00:12:15.996
Here you come haulin' ass.

269
00:12:16.031 --> 00:12:18.265
I looked all around to make
sure there was no one there,

270
00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:20.501
particularly no children.

271
00:12:20.535 --> 00:12:22.336
There was nobody around.

272
00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:23.704
No, just me.

273
00:12:23.738 --> 00:12:26.740
Well, I don't know
where you could have been.

274
00:12:26.775 --> 00:12:29.476
Fixing to get spooky
on me here, Professor?

275
00:12:29.511 --> 00:12:31.045
Maybe I was behind
a post or something.

276
00:12:31.079 --> 00:12:32.980
There wasn't any post.

277
00:12:33.014 --> 00:12:35.282
So what're you saying...
You're looking at some big black angel

278
00:12:35.317 --> 00:12:37.985
got sent down here to snatch
your honky ass out of there

279
00:12:38.019 --> 00:12:40.020
at the last possible minute and
save you from destruction?

280
00:12:40.055 --> 00:12:41.622
No, I don't think that.

281
00:12:41.656 --> 00:12:43.857
- Such a thing ain't possible?
- No, it isn't.

282
00:12:43.892 --> 00:12:45.326
Well, you're the one
suggested it.

283
00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:46.894
I never suggested
any such thing.

284
00:12:46.928 --> 00:12:48.662
You're the one who put in
the stuff about angels.

285
00:12:48.697 --> 00:12:51.398
I never said anything about angels.
I don't believe in angels.

286
00:12:51.433 --> 00:12:54.168
Well, what is it
you believe in?

287
00:12:54.202 --> 00:12:55.903
A lot of things.

288
00:12:55.937 --> 00:12:57.404
All right.

289
00:12:57.439 --> 00:12:59.807
- All right what?
- All right, what things?

290
00:12:59.841 --> 00:13:03.110
- I believe in things.
- Give me a for instance.

291
00:13:03.144 --> 00:13:06.680
Um, cultural things,
for instance,

292
00:13:06.715 --> 00:13:09.683
books, music, art,
things like that.

293
00:13:09.718 --> 00:13:11.652
All right.

294
00:13:11.686 --> 00:13:14.688
Those are the things
that have value to me.

295
00:13:14.723 --> 00:13:16.957
They're the foundations
of civilization.

296
00:13:16.992 --> 00:13:18.959
Well, they used to
have value to me.

297
00:13:18.994 --> 00:13:21.629
They don't have so much
value anymore, I guess.

298
00:13:21.663 --> 00:13:22.997
What happened to them?

299
00:13:23.031 --> 00:13:25.666
People stopped valuing them.
I stopped valuing them

300
00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:28.269
to a certain extent.

301
00:13:28.303 --> 00:13:30.271
I'm not sure
I can tell you why.

302
00:13:30.305 --> 00:13:33.407
That world is largely gone now.

303
00:13:33.441 --> 00:13:35.476
Soon it will be wholly gone.

304
00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:38.078
I'm not sure I'm
following you, Professor.

305
00:13:38.113 --> 00:13:40.314
There's nothing to follow.
It's all right.

306
00:13:40.348 --> 00:13:42.983
The things I loved
were very frail,

307
00:13:43.018 --> 00:13:44.918
very fragile.

308
00:13:44.953 --> 00:13:47.488
I didn't know that.
I thought they were indestructible.

309
00:13:47.522 --> 00:13:49.256
They weren't.

310
00:13:49.291 --> 00:13:51.425
And that's what sent you off
the edge of the platform?

311
00:13:51.459 --> 00:13:52.860
It wasn't nothing personal?

312
00:13:52.894 --> 00:13:55.029
Oh, it's personal.
That's what an education does.

313
00:13:55.063 --> 00:13:57.798
It makes the world personal.

314
00:13:57.832 --> 00:14:00.734
Well, them's some very
powerful words, Professor.

315
00:14:00.769 --> 00:14:03.937
And I can't say that I got
an answer to none of that.

316
00:14:03.972 --> 00:14:06.106
And it might be that
there ain't no answer.

317
00:14:06.141 --> 00:14:08.575
But still I got to ask.

318
00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:11.412
What's the use of having
notions such as them

319
00:14:11.446 --> 00:14:13.580
if they won't keep you
glued down to the platform

320
00:14:13.615 --> 00:14:16.450
when the Sunset Limited is
coming through at 80mph?

321
00:14:16.484 --> 00:14:18.552
- Good question.
- I thought so.

322
00:14:18.586 --> 00:14:21.121
I don't have an answer
to any of that either.

323
00:14:21.156 --> 00:14:24.091
Maybe it's not logical.
I don't know. I don't care.

324
00:14:24.125 --> 00:14:26.060
I've been asked,
didn't I think it odd

325
00:14:26.094 --> 00:14:28.829
that I should be around to
witness the death of everything?

326
00:14:28.863 --> 00:14:30.831
I do think it's odd.

327
00:14:30.865 --> 00:14:33.534
But that doesn't mean
it isn't so.

328
00:14:33.568 --> 00:14:35.436
Somebody has to be here.

329
00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:38.072
But <i>you</i> don't intend
to hang around for it?

330
00:14:38.106 --> 00:14:39.640
No, I don't.

331
00:14:41.476 --> 00:14:43.377
Let me see if I got
this straight.

332
00:14:43.411 --> 00:14:46.347
You're saying that
all this culture stuff

333
00:14:46.381 --> 00:14:49.583
is the only thing between
you and the Sunset Limited.

334
00:14:49.617 --> 00:14:51.618
- It's a lot.
- But it busted out on you.

335
00:14:51.653 --> 00:14:52.853
Yes.

336
00:14:52.887 --> 00:14:55.322
You're a culture junkie.

337
00:14:58.159 --> 00:14:59.860
Maybe you're right.

338
00:14:59.894 --> 00:15:03.964
Maybe I have no beliefs.

339
00:15:03.998 --> 00:15:07.668
I believe
in the Sunset Limited.

340
00:15:07.702 --> 00:15:09.670
Damn, Professor.

341
00:15:09.704 --> 00:15:11.438
Damn indeed.

342
00:15:11.473 --> 00:15:13.674
No beliefs?

343
00:15:13.708 --> 00:15:17.678
The things I believed in

344
00:15:17.712 --> 00:15:20.814
no longer exist.

345
00:15:20.849 --> 00:15:23.150
It's foolish
to pretend they do.

346
00:15:23.184 --> 00:15:26.653
Western civilization
finally went up in smoke

347
00:15:26.688 --> 00:15:28.622
in the chimneys of Dachau,

348
00:15:28.656 --> 00:15:31.625
and I was too infatuated
to see it.

349
00:15:31.659 --> 00:15:33.994
I see it now.

350
00:15:37.899 --> 00:15:39.833
Hoo!

351
00:15:39.868 --> 00:15:41.902
You're a challenge,
Professor, you know that?

352
00:15:41.936 --> 00:15:43.604
Well, there's no reason for you

353
00:15:43.638 --> 00:15:47.207
to become involved
in my problems.

354
00:15:47.242 --> 00:15:49.710
I should go.

355
00:15:53.248 --> 00:15:55.783
- Have you got any friends?
- No.

356
00:15:55.817 --> 00:15:57.885
- Come on, Professor, not one?
- Not really, no.

357
00:15:57.919 --> 00:15:59.787
- Tell me about that one.
- What one?

358
00:15:59.821 --> 00:16:03.257
- The "not really" one.
- I have a friend at the university...

359
00:16:03.291 --> 00:16:06.059
Not a close friend.
We have lunch from time to time.

360
00:16:06.094 --> 00:16:09.196
But that's about as good as it gets?

361
00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:10.330
Yes.

362
00:16:10.365 --> 00:16:12.199
What'd you do to him?

363
00:16:12.233 --> 00:16:14.234
- What'd I do to him?
- <i>Yeah.</i>

364
00:16:14.269 --> 00:16:16.236
I didn't do anything to him.

365
00:16:16.271 --> 00:16:18.238
What makes you think
I did something to him?

366
00:16:18.273 --> 00:16:19.506
I don't know.
Did you?

367
00:16:19.574 --> 00:16:20.774
No.

368
00:16:20.809 --> 00:16:22.876
What is it you think
I did to him?

369
00:16:22.911 --> 00:16:24.611
I don't know.
I want you to tell me.

370
00:16:24.646 --> 00:16:27.047
There's nothing to tell.

371
00:16:27.081 --> 00:16:29.316
You didn't leave him
a note or nothing

372
00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:31.418
- to tell him you've taken the train?
- <i>No.</i>

373
00:16:31.453 --> 00:16:33.387
- Your best friend?
- He's not my best friend.

374
00:16:33.421 --> 00:16:35.389
I thought we just got
done deciding he was.

375
00:16:35.423 --> 00:16:38.125
You just got done
deciding he...

376
00:16:38.159 --> 00:16:40.694
Did you ever tell him
you was thinking about this?

377
00:16:40.728 --> 00:16:43.263
- No. Why should I?
- 'Cause he's your best friend.

378
00:16:43.298 --> 00:16:45.232
He's... I told you,
we're not all that close.

379
00:16:45.266 --> 00:16:46.967
- You're not all that close?
- <i>No.</i>

380
00:16:47.001 --> 00:16:48.869
He's your best friend,
only you ain't all that close?

381
00:16:48.903 --> 00:16:50.337
If you'd like.

382
00:16:50.371 --> 00:16:53.307
Not the way you'd want to
bother him with a little thing

383
00:16:53.341 --> 00:16:54.875
like suicide?

384
00:16:56.144 --> 00:16:58.011
Look,

385
00:16:58.046 --> 00:17:03.016
suppose I were
to give you my word

386
00:17:03.051 --> 00:17:05.652
that I would just go home

387
00:17:05.687 --> 00:17:09.089
and I wouldn't try
to kill myself en route.

388
00:17:23.671 --> 00:17:27.341
Suppose I was
to give you <i>my</i> word

389
00:17:27.375 --> 00:17:30.677
that I wouldn't listen
to none of your bullshit.

390
00:17:33.081 --> 00:17:35.749
So what am I, a prisoner here?

391
00:17:35.783 --> 00:17:37.551
You know better than that.

392
00:17:39.454 --> 00:17:41.855
You was a prisoner
before you got here...

393
00:17:44.459 --> 00:17:47.261
A death row prisoner.

394
00:17:50.031 --> 00:17:52.299
What did your daddy do?

395
00:17:52.333 --> 00:17:53.433
What?

396
00:17:53.468 --> 00:17:56.103
I said what did
your daddy do...

397
00:17:56.137 --> 00:17:57.938
What kind of work?

398
00:18:02.377 --> 00:18:03.510
He was a lawyer.

399
00:18:03.545 --> 00:18:04.845
- A lawyer?
- Yeah.

400
00:18:04.879 --> 00:18:06.813
What kind of law did he do?

401
00:18:06.848 --> 00:18:10.050
He was a government lawyer.
He didn't do criminal law or anything like that.

402
00:18:10.084 --> 00:18:12.452
What would be a thing
like criminal law?

403
00:18:12.487 --> 00:18:14.354
I don't know.
Divorce law maybe.

404
00:18:14.389 --> 00:18:16.356
Mm-hmm.

405
00:18:16.391 --> 00:18:18.892
Maybe you got a point.

406
00:18:18.927 --> 00:18:20.427
What'd he die of?

407
00:18:20.461 --> 00:18:22.095
Who said he was dead?

408
00:18:22.130 --> 00:18:24.865
- Is he dead?
- <i>Yes.</i>

409
00:18:24.899 --> 00:18:26.533
What did he die of?

410
00:18:26.568 --> 00:18:27.834
Cancer.

411
00:18:27.869 --> 00:18:29.703
Cancer. So he was
sick for a while?

412
00:18:29.737 --> 00:18:31.238
Yes, he was.

413
00:18:31.272 --> 00:18:33.407
- Did you go see him?
- No.

414
00:18:33.441 --> 00:18:35.876
- How come?
- I didn't want to.

415
00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:37.778
How come you didn't want to?

416
00:18:37.812 --> 00:18:41.148
I don't know.
I just didn't.

417
00:18:41.182 --> 00:18:43.250
Maybe I didn't want to
remember him that way.

418
00:18:43.284 --> 00:18:45.452
Mm, bullshit.

419
00:18:45.486 --> 00:18:46.987
Did he ask you to come?

420
00:18:47.021 --> 00:18:48.555
No.

421
00:18:48.590 --> 00:18:50.891
- But your mama did.
- She may have. I don't remember.

422
00:18:50.925 --> 00:18:53.126
Come on, Professor.
You know she asked you to come.

423
00:18:53.161 --> 00:18:54.461
Okay, yes.

424
00:18:54.495 --> 00:18:56.830
- What did you tell her?
- I said I would.

425
00:18:56.864 --> 00:18:58.832
- But you didn't?
- No.

426
00:18:58.866 --> 00:19:01.668
- How come?
- He died.

427
00:19:01.703 --> 00:19:03.437
No no, that ain't it.

428
00:19:03.471 --> 00:19:05.372
You had time to go see him,
but you didn't do it.

429
00:19:05.406 --> 00:19:07.040
I suppose.

430
00:19:07.075 --> 00:19:08.542
You waited till he was dead.

431
00:19:08.576 --> 00:19:10.177
Okay,

432
00:19:10.211 --> 00:19:12.512
so I didn't go see my father.

433
00:19:14.115 --> 00:19:17.117
Your father is laying on his
deathbed dying of cancer.

434
00:19:17.151 --> 00:19:19.219
Your mama's sitting there
with him, holding his hand.

435
00:19:19.253 --> 00:19:21.088
He's in all kind of pain.

436
00:19:21.122 --> 00:19:24.458
They ask you to come see him
one last time before he died.

437
00:19:24.492 --> 00:19:26.426
And you tell them,
"No, I ain't coming."

438
00:19:26.461 --> 00:19:28.528
Please tell me I got
some part of this wrong.

439
00:19:28.563 --> 00:19:30.430
If that's the way
you want to put it.

440
00:19:30.465 --> 00:19:32.599
- Well, how would you put it?
- I don't know.

441
00:19:32.634 --> 00:19:34.267
Well, that's the way
it is then, ain't it?

442
00:19:34.302 --> 00:19:37.004
- I suppose.
- No, ain't no suppose.

443
00:19:37.038 --> 00:19:39.740
- Is it or ain't it?
- Yes.

444
00:19:42.377 --> 00:19:43.543
Whoo!

445
00:19:43.578 --> 00:19:45.846
Let me see if I can find
my train schedule,

446
00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:49.082
see when that next
uptown express is due.

447
00:19:52.387 --> 00:19:55.155
I'm not sure I see the humor.

448
00:19:55.189 --> 00:19:58.091
I'm glad to hear you
say that, Professor,

449
00:19:58.126 --> 00:19:59.393
'cause I ain't sure either.

450
00:19:59.427 --> 00:20:02.963
<i>I get more amazed</i>
<i>by the minute.</i>

451
00:20:07.468 --> 00:20:09.469
How come you can't
see yourself, honey?

452
00:20:09.504 --> 00:20:10.937
You're clear as glass.

453
00:20:10.972 --> 00:20:13.407
I can see the wheels in
there turning, the gears.

454
00:20:13.441 --> 00:20:16.009
I can see light too...
good light,

455
00:20:16.044 --> 00:20:19.079
true light.
Can't you see it?

456
00:20:19.113 --> 00:20:21.314
No, I can't.

457
00:20:22.383 --> 00:20:24.985
Well, bless you, brother.

458
00:20:25.019 --> 00:20:27.087
Bless and keep you,

459
00:20:27.121 --> 00:20:29.089
'cause it's there.

460
00:20:42.303 --> 00:20:44.271
When were you
in the penitentiary?

461
00:20:46.841 --> 00:20:49.176
- A long time ago.
- What were you in for?

462
00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:52.245
- Murder.
- Really?

463
00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:54.781
Now who would claim
to be a murderer

464
00:20:54.816 --> 00:20:56.917
that wasn't one?

465
00:21:04.525 --> 00:21:06.493
You called it the jailhouse.

466
00:21:06.527 --> 00:21:08.061
<i>Yeah.</i>

467
00:21:08.096 --> 00:21:10.163
Do most black people
call the penitentiary

468
00:21:10.198 --> 00:21:12.532
the jailhouse?

469
00:21:12.567 --> 00:21:15.335
No, just us
old country niggers.

470
00:21:17.705 --> 00:21:19.973
We make a point to call
things what they is.

471
00:21:20.007 --> 00:21:23.510
<i>I'd hate to think how many names there is</i>
<i>for the jailhouse.</i>

472
00:21:26.380 --> 00:21:28.014
<i>I'd hate to have</i>
<i>to count them.</i>

473
00:21:28.049 --> 00:21:30.016
Do you have a lot
of jailhouse stories?

474
00:21:30.051 --> 00:21:32.252
- Jailhouse stories?
- Yeah.

475
00:21:34.288 --> 00:21:36.223
I don't know.

476
00:21:36.257 --> 00:21:38.225
I used to tell
jailhouse stories,

477
00:21:38.259 --> 00:21:41.695
but they kind of
lost their charm.

478
00:21:41.729 --> 00:21:43.530
Why don't we talk
about something

479
00:21:43.564 --> 00:21:45.065
that's a little more cheerful?

480
00:21:45.099 --> 00:21:46.900
You ever been married?

481
00:21:46.934 --> 00:21:48.001
Married?

482
00:21:48.035 --> 00:21:49.970
- Yeah.
- <i>Oh man.</i>

483
00:21:50.004 --> 00:21:51.972
What?

484
00:21:52.006 --> 00:21:55.542
Oh...

485
00:21:55.576 --> 00:21:58.745
Maybe we ought to take another
look at them jailhouse stories.

486
00:21:58.780 --> 00:22:01.414
Oh my.

487
00:22:01.449 --> 00:22:02.682
Hoo!

488
00:22:02.717 --> 00:22:04.050
Do you have any children?

489
00:22:04.085 --> 00:22:06.353
No, Professor.
I ain't got nobody.

490
00:22:06.387 --> 00:22:08.355
Everybody in my family's dead.

491
00:22:08.389 --> 00:22:11.591
I had two boys, but they
died a long time ago.

492
00:22:11.626 --> 00:22:15.529
Just about everybody I ever
knowed has died, for that matter.

493
00:22:15.563 --> 00:22:17.497
You ought to think about that.

494
00:22:17.532 --> 00:22:19.266
I might be a hazard
to your health.

495
00:22:19.300 --> 00:22:21.568
Were you always
in a lot of trouble?

496
00:22:21.602 --> 00:22:23.036
Yeah, I was.

497
00:22:23.070 --> 00:22:25.305
I liked it.

498
00:22:25.339 --> 00:22:27.674
I think I still do.

499
00:22:27.708 --> 00:22:29.709
I done seven years hard time.

500
00:22:29.744 --> 00:22:31.778
Could have done a lot more.
Hurt a lot of people.

501
00:22:31.813 --> 00:22:34.848
I used to smack 'em around,
and they wouldn't get up no more.

502
00:22:34.882 --> 00:22:36.883
- But you don't get in trouble now?
- No.

503
00:22:36.918 --> 00:22:38.418
But you still like it?

504
00:22:38.452 --> 00:22:41.555
Well, maybe I'm just
condemned to it,

505
00:22:41.589 --> 00:22:43.924
bit in the ass by my own karma.

506
00:22:43.958 --> 00:22:45.826
But I'm on the other side now.

507
00:22:45.860 --> 00:22:47.661
You want to help folk
that's in trouble,

508
00:22:47.695 --> 00:22:49.696
you pretty much got to go
where the trouble's at.

509
00:22:49.730 --> 00:22:52.532
You ain't got a lot of choice.

510
00:22:52.567 --> 00:22:53.900
<i>How long have you</i>
<i>been here?</i>

511
00:22:53.935 --> 00:22:56.570
<i>Oh, six...</i>
<i>going on seven years.</i>

512
00:22:56.604 --> 00:22:58.205
I don't understand
why you live here.

513
00:22:58.239 --> 00:23:00.140
As opposed to where?

514
00:23:00.174 --> 00:23:01.241
Anywhere.

515
00:23:01.275 --> 00:23:04.611
Well, this pretty much
<i>is</i> anywhere.

516
00:23:04.645 --> 00:23:08.081
I could live in another
building, I suppose,

517
00:23:08.115 --> 00:23:10.650
but it's all right.

518
00:23:10.685 --> 00:23:13.520
I got my bedroom over yonder

519
00:23:13.554 --> 00:23:15.488
where I can get away,

520
00:23:15.523 --> 00:23:17.490
got a sofa where
folk can crash...

521
00:23:17.525 --> 00:23:19.593
junkies and crackheads mostly.

522
00:23:19.627 --> 00:23:22.596
Of course they're gonna walk
off with all your portables,

523
00:23:22.630 --> 00:23:25.365
so you can't own nothing.
But that's good.

524
00:23:25.399 --> 00:23:27.367
You hang out
with the right crowd,

525
00:23:27.401 --> 00:23:30.103
<i>you pretty much get over</i>
<i>all your worldly cravings.</i>

526
00:23:30.137 --> 00:23:32.105
They took the refrigerator
one time,

527
00:23:32.139 --> 00:23:34.107
but somebody caught them
out there on the stairs,

528
00:23:34.141 --> 00:23:35.775
made them bring it back up.

529
00:23:35.810 --> 00:23:37.944
Now I got that big
sucker over yonder...

530
00:23:37.979 --> 00:23:40.146
traded up.

531
00:23:42.550 --> 00:23:44.951
Only thing I miss

532
00:23:44.986 --> 00:23:46.319
is the music.

533
00:23:46.354 --> 00:23:49.522
<i>I aim to get me</i>
<i>a steel door.</i>

534
00:23:49.557 --> 00:23:52.092
Then I can have me
some music here.

535
00:23:52.126 --> 00:23:56.229
But you got to buy the door and
the frame at the same time.

536
00:23:56.264 --> 00:23:58.198
I'm working on that.

537
00:23:59.967 --> 00:24:02.836
I don't care nothing
about television,

538
00:24:02.870 --> 00:24:05.972
but I sure do miss the music.

539
00:24:06.007 --> 00:24:09.676
You don't think
this is a terrible place?

540
00:24:09.710 --> 00:24:12.145
A terrible place?

541
00:24:12.179 --> 00:24:13.580
Yes.

542
00:24:13.614 --> 00:24:15.015
What are you talking about?

543
00:24:15.049 --> 00:24:18.184
This place. It's a horrible place
full of horrible people.

544
00:24:18.219 --> 00:24:19.419
Oh my.

545
00:24:19.453 --> 00:24:21.688
You must know that these
people are not worth saving

546
00:24:21.722 --> 00:24:24.891
even if they could be saved,
which they can't. You must know that.

547
00:24:24.926 --> 00:24:27.627
Well, I like a challenge.

548
00:24:27.662 --> 00:24:30.330
I started a ministry
'fore I got out of prison.

549
00:24:30.364 --> 00:24:31.998
Now that was a challenge.

550
00:24:32.033 --> 00:24:34.000
There was a lot of
brothers who showed up.

551
00:24:34.035 --> 00:24:35.568
They ain't care
nothing about it.

552
00:24:35.603 --> 00:24:37.971
They ain't really care nothing
about the word of God.

553
00:24:38.005 --> 00:24:39.406
They just wanted it
on the resume.

554
00:24:39.440 --> 00:24:41.074
- Resume?
- Resume.

555
00:24:41.108 --> 00:24:44.177
You got some brothers in there
had done some real bad shit.

556
00:24:44.211 --> 00:24:46.980
And they weren't sorry about a
damn thing except getting caught.

557
00:24:47.014 --> 00:24:51.117
But the funny thing was a lot
of them did believe in God,

558
00:24:51.152 --> 00:24:54.254
even more so than a lot of these
people out here on the outside.

559
00:24:54.288 --> 00:24:56.022
I know I did.

560
00:24:56.057 --> 00:24:57.724
You ought to think
about that, Professor.

561
00:24:57.758 --> 00:25:00.126
- I think I'd better go.
- Oh, hey hey.

562
00:25:00.161 --> 00:25:02.128
You ain't got to go, Professor.

563
00:25:02.163 --> 00:25:04.130
Let me ask you something.

564
00:25:04.165 --> 00:25:06.232
You ever had one of them days

565
00:25:06.267 --> 00:25:10.203
where everything was just
weird all the way around

566
00:25:10.237 --> 00:25:12.872
when things just kind of
fell into place?

567
00:25:12.907 --> 00:25:14.140
I'm not sure what you mean.

568
00:25:14.175 --> 00:25:17.177
Just one of them days
kind of magic,

569
00:25:17.211 --> 00:25:20.780
one of them days where
everything turned out right.

570
00:25:20.815 --> 00:25:22.582
I don't know.
Maybe.Why?

571
00:25:22.616 --> 00:25:25.018
Well, I'm just wondering
if it ain't been

572
00:25:25.052 --> 00:25:28.688
maybe sort of long
dry spell for you

573
00:25:28.723 --> 00:25:30.690
till you took up
with the notion

574
00:25:30.725 --> 00:25:32.125
that that's the way
the world is.

575
00:25:32.159 --> 00:25:33.660
- The way the world is?
- Yeah.

576
00:25:33.694 --> 00:25:36.096
- And how is that?
- You know, long and dry.

577
00:25:36.130 --> 00:25:38.064
But the point is

578
00:25:38.099 --> 00:25:40.100
that even if you feel that way,

579
00:25:40.134 --> 00:25:42.068
what you got to understand

580
00:25:42.103 --> 00:25:44.604
is that the sun don't shine up
the same dog's ass every day.

581
00:25:44.638 --> 00:25:46.573
You understand what I'm saying?

582
00:25:46.607 --> 00:25:49.075
If what you're saying is that I'm simply
having a bad day, that's ridiculous.

583
00:25:49.110 --> 00:25:51.277
I ain't saying you're having
a bad day, Professor.

584
00:25:51.312 --> 00:25:52.612
I'm saying you're
having a bad life.

585
00:25:52.646 --> 00:25:54.614
You think I should
change my life?

586
00:25:54.648 --> 00:25:56.616
- What, you shitting me?
- I have to go.

587
00:25:56.650 --> 00:25:59.185
Look, just hang with me
a little while longer.

588
00:26:01.455 --> 00:26:03.423
What about my jailhouse story?

589
00:26:03.457 --> 00:26:06.192
You don't need
no jailhouse story.

590
00:26:06.227 --> 00:26:07.560
Why not?

591
00:26:07.595 --> 00:26:10.330
Well, you're suspicious
about everything already.

592
00:26:10.364 --> 00:26:12.465
You think I'm trying to
put you in the trick bag.

593
00:26:12.500 --> 00:26:13.566
And you're not?

594
00:26:13.601 --> 00:26:15.902
Well yeah, but I don't
want you to know it.

595
00:26:15.936 --> 00:26:17.704
Well, in any case,
I have to go.

596
00:26:17.738 --> 00:26:19.706
You ain't ready to go back
out there on them streets.

597
00:26:19.740 --> 00:26:22.776
I have to. - I know you ain't got
nothing you got to do.

598
00:26:22.810 --> 00:26:25.779
- How do you know that?
- 'Cause you ain't even supposed to be here.

599
00:26:25.813 --> 00:26:29.182
I see your point.

600
00:26:29.216 --> 00:26:31.217
What if I tell you
a jailhouse story?

601
00:26:31.252 --> 00:26:33.319
Will you stay then?

602
00:26:36.290 --> 00:26:38.591
All right.
I'll stay a while.

603
00:26:38.626 --> 00:26:41.261
All right!
The man says all right.

604
00:26:41.295 --> 00:26:44.030
Okay, here go
my jailhouse story.

605
00:26:44.065 --> 00:26:45.632
Is it a true story?

606
00:26:45.666 --> 00:26:47.067
Oh yeah, it's a true story.

607
00:26:47.101 --> 00:26:49.035
I don't know no other kind.

608
00:26:49.070 --> 00:26:52.405
One day I'm in the chow line
and I'm getting my chow

609
00:26:52.440 --> 00:26:54.407
when the nigger
in the line behind me

610
00:26:54.442 --> 00:26:56.810
gets into it with the server,
says the beans is cold.

611
00:26:56.844 --> 00:26:59.512
So he throws the ladle
down in the beans.

612
00:26:59.547 --> 00:27:00.980
Well, when he done that

613
00:27:01.015 --> 00:27:02.982
there was beans
splashed up on me.

614
00:27:03.017 --> 00:27:05.952
Well, I didn't want to get
into it over no beans,

615
00:27:05.986 --> 00:27:08.221
but it did kinda
piss me off some.

616
00:27:08.255 --> 00:27:10.090
I'd just put on
a clean uniform...

617
00:27:10.124 --> 00:27:12.425
you know, khakis,
shirt and trousers.

618
00:27:12.460 --> 00:27:14.160
And you only get two a week.

619
00:27:14.195 --> 00:27:16.096
So I said something to him

620
00:27:16.130 --> 00:27:19.032
like "Hey man, watch it"
or something like that.

621
00:27:19.066 --> 00:27:21.601
But I went on.
I'm telling myself,

622
00:27:21.635 --> 00:27:24.637
"Just let it go.
Just let it go."

623
00:27:24.672 --> 00:27:27.040
But then the nigger
said something.

624
00:27:27.074 --> 00:27:29.709
So I turned back.

625
00:27:29.743 --> 00:27:31.377
And when I done that,

626
00:27:31.412 --> 00:27:33.246
he stuck a knife in me.

627
00:27:33.280 --> 00:27:36.049
I ain't even see it coming.
Blood's just flying everywhere.

628
00:27:36.083 --> 00:27:38.151
This wasn't no
jailhouse shiv neither.

629
00:27:38.185 --> 00:27:40.753
This was one of them
Italian switchblades...

630
00:27:40.788 --> 00:27:42.655
One of them
black-and-silver jobs.

631
00:27:42.690 --> 00:27:44.491
Well, I ain't do a thing
but duck

632
00:27:44.525 --> 00:27:46.759
and step back under the rail.

633
00:27:46.794 --> 00:27:49.929
And I reached out and I got hold of
this table leg.

634
00:27:49.964 --> 00:27:53.366
And it come off in my hand
just as easy.

635
00:27:53.400 --> 00:27:55.201
It had this long screw

636
00:27:55.236 --> 00:27:57.237
sticking out the end of it.

637
00:27:57.271 --> 00:28:00.707
And I went to wailing
on that nigger's head!

638
00:28:00.741 --> 00:28:02.442
And I ain't quit!

639
00:28:02.476 --> 00:28:04.878
I ain't quit
till you couldn't tell

640
00:28:04.912 --> 00:28:07.113
it was a head no more!

641
00:28:07.148 --> 00:28:08.848
That screw was stick
in his head

642
00:28:08.883 --> 00:28:11.784
and I'd have to put my foot
on him to pull it out.

643
00:28:15.623 --> 00:28:18.558
- What'd he say?
- What'd he say?

644
00:28:18.592 --> 00:28:20.160
I mean in the line.
What did he say?

645
00:28:20.194 --> 00:28:22.295
- I ain't gonna repeat it.
- That doesn't seem fair.

646
00:28:22.329 --> 00:28:23.696
- Don't seem fair?
- No.

647
00:28:23.731 --> 00:28:25.365
Here I am telling you

648
00:28:25.399 --> 00:28:28.368
a bona fide blood-and-guts
tale from the big house,

649
00:28:28.402 --> 00:28:30.303
the genuine article,
and I can't get you

650
00:28:30.337 --> 00:28:32.238
to fill in the blanks
about what the nigger said?

651
00:28:32.273 --> 00:28:34.207
- Do you have to use that word?
- Use that word?

652
00:28:34.241 --> 00:28:36.776
Yes. - We ain't making much
progress here, is we?

653
00:28:36.810 --> 00:28:38.545
It just seems unnecessary.

654
00:28:38.579 --> 00:28:40.180
You don't want
to hear "nigger,"

655
00:28:40.214 --> 00:28:42.215
but you about to bail out on
me 'cause I won't tell you

656
00:28:42.249 --> 00:28:44.484
some ugly shit the nigger said?
Is you sure about this?

657
00:28:44.518 --> 00:28:46.352
I just don't see why you
have to use that word.

658
00:28:46.387 --> 00:28:48.521
It's my story, ain't it?

659
00:28:48.556 --> 00:28:51.257
Anyway, I don't remember there being
no African-Americans

660
00:28:51.292 --> 00:28:52.792
or people of color in there.

661
00:28:52.826 --> 00:28:57.263
To my recollection there was
only a bunch of niggers!

662
00:28:58.165 --> 00:28:59.566
Go ahead.

663
00:29:01.335 --> 00:29:03.102
Now at some point

664
00:29:03.137 --> 00:29:05.672
I musta pulled
the knife out and...

665
00:29:05.739 --> 00:29:07.307
and dropped on the floor
somewhere.

666
00:29:07.341 --> 00:29:10.777
Meanwhile, I'm still wailing
on this nigger's head,

667
00:29:10.811 --> 00:29:13.446
but his friend has got
ahold of me from behind.

668
00:29:13.480 --> 00:29:16.049
But I got one hand on the rail

669
00:29:16.083 --> 00:29:18.251
so I ain't going nowhere.

670
00:29:18.285 --> 00:29:21.020
But what I don't know
is that this dude

671
00:29:21.055 --> 00:29:22.722
musta picked up the knife

672
00:29:22.756 --> 00:29:24.624
and he's trying to gut me.

673
00:29:24.658 --> 00:29:27.193
I felt the blood.
I turned around

674
00:29:27.228 --> 00:29:29.195
and I bust him in the head.

675
00:29:29.230 --> 00:29:31.364
And he went skittering off
across the floor.

676
00:29:31.398 --> 00:29:34.200
And by now somebody
done pushed the button

677
00:29:34.235 --> 00:29:35.768
and the alarm is going.

678
00:29:35.803 --> 00:29:37.670
Everybody's down on the floor

679
00:29:37.705 --> 00:29:39.973
and we in lockdown.

680
00:29:40.007 --> 00:29:42.141
The guard up on the rail...

681
00:29:42.176 --> 00:29:44.711
He got his shotgun
pointed at me

682
00:29:44.745 --> 00:29:46.246
and he's hollering,

683
00:29:46.280 --> 00:29:48.982
"You! Get on the floor
and drop the weapon!"

684
00:29:49.016 --> 00:29:50.984
He's about to shoot me

685
00:29:51.018 --> 00:29:52.318
when the lieutenant tells him,

686
00:29:52.353 --> 00:29:56.322
"Hold your fire!
You! Throw down that club!"

687
00:29:56.357 --> 00:29:58.758
I looked around,

688
00:29:58.792 --> 00:30:00.994
and I was the only one
standing.

689
00:30:01.028 --> 00:30:03.796
I see the nigger's feet sticking
out from under the counter

690
00:30:03.831 --> 00:30:05.331
where he'd done crawled.

691
00:30:05.366 --> 00:30:09.836
So I throwed the thing down.

692
00:30:09.870 --> 00:30:12.238
I don't remember
much after that.

693
00:30:12.273 --> 00:30:14.641
They...
they told me

694
00:30:14.675 --> 00:30:19.345
I had lost almost
half my blood.

695
00:30:19.380 --> 00:30:21.414
I remember
slipping around in it,

696
00:30:21.448 --> 00:30:25.785
but I thought it was
the other dude's.

697
00:30:30.557 --> 00:30:32.058
Did the man die?

698
00:30:32.092 --> 00:30:35.395
No, he didn't.

699
00:30:35.429 --> 00:30:38.197
Everybody lived.

700
00:30:38.232 --> 00:30:41.000
They thought he was dead,
but he wasn't.

701
00:30:41.035 --> 00:30:44.203
He wasn't right after that,

702
00:30:44.238 --> 00:30:47.840
so I ain't really had
no more trouble out of him.

703
00:30:47.875 --> 00:30:50.943
He walked around with his
head kinda to the side,

704
00:30:50.978 --> 00:30:52.745
lost one eye,

705
00:30:52.780 --> 00:30:55.648
arm's hanging down,
didn't talk right.

706
00:30:55.683 --> 00:30:58.951
They shipped him off
to another facility.

707
00:30:58.986 --> 00:31:01.154
But that's not the whole story.

708
00:31:01.188 --> 00:31:03.723
- No, it ain't.
- So what happened?

709
00:31:03.757 --> 00:31:05.591
I woke up in the infirmary.

710
00:31:05.626 --> 00:31:07.527
They had already done
operated on me.

711
00:31:07.561 --> 00:31:09.829
My spleen was cut open,

712
00:31:09.863 --> 00:31:11.898
liver, I don't know what all.

713
00:31:11.932 --> 00:31:14.734
I come pretty close to dying.

714
00:31:14.768 --> 00:31:17.837
I had 280 stitches
holding me together

715
00:31:17.871 --> 00:31:22.041
and, whoo, I was hurting.

716
00:31:22.076 --> 00:31:24.344
I didn't know you could
hurt that much.

717
00:31:24.378 --> 00:31:26.379
And still they had me
in leg irons

718
00:31:26.413 --> 00:31:29.248
and had me handcuffed to the
bed, if you can believe that.

719
00:31:29.283 --> 00:31:31.617
Now as I lay there,

720
00:31:31.652 --> 00:31:34.520
I heard this voice
just as clear,

721
00:31:34.555 --> 00:31:36.122
couldn't have been no clearer.

722
00:31:36.156 --> 00:31:38.257
And the voice said,

723
00:31:38.292 --> 00:31:42.362
"If it was not
r the grace of God

724
00:31:42.396 --> 00:31:44.497
you would not be here."

725
00:31:44.531 --> 00:31:47.133
Man, I tried to sit up

726
00:31:47.167 --> 00:31:49.635
and look around,
but I couldn't.

727
00:31:49.670 --> 00:31:52.271
Wasn't no need to nohow,
'cause wasn't nobody there.

728
00:31:52.306 --> 00:31:54.841
I mean, there was somebody
there all right,

729
00:31:54.875 --> 00:31:57.543
but wasn't no need for me looking
around to see if I could see him.

730
00:31:57.578 --> 00:32:00.213
You don't think this is
a strange kind of story?

731
00:32:00.247 --> 00:32:03.249
Yeah, I do think
it's a strange kind of story.

732
00:32:03.283 --> 00:32:06.619
No, what I mean is that you
didn't feel sorry for this man.

733
00:32:06.653 --> 00:32:08.588
Oh, you getting ahead
of the story.

734
00:32:08.622 --> 00:32:11.891
The story of how a fellow inmate
became a crippled one-eyed halfwit

735
00:32:11.925 --> 00:32:13.626
so that you could find God.

736
00:32:13.660 --> 00:32:15.395
- Whoa-ho.
- Well, isn't it?

737
00:32:15.429 --> 00:32:17.497
- I don't know.
- You hadn't thought of it that way?

738
00:32:17.531 --> 00:32:19.932
Oh yeah, hell,
I thought of it that way.

739
00:32:19.967 --> 00:32:22.568
Well, isn't that
the true story?

740
00:32:22.603 --> 00:32:25.671
Look, Professor, I don't want to
get on the wrong side of you.

741
00:32:25.706 --> 00:32:27.673
And you seem to have
a powerful wish

742
00:32:27.708 --> 00:32:29.342
for that to be the story,

743
00:32:29.376 --> 00:32:33.146
so I got to say that is
one way of looking at it.

744
00:32:33.180 --> 00:32:36.082
I gotta concede that.
Gotta keep you interested.

745
00:32:36.116 --> 00:32:38.651
- <i>String me along.</i>
- You all right with that?

746
00:32:38.685 --> 00:32:41.888
And then put me in the...
What is it? The trick bag?

747
00:32:41.922 --> 00:32:43.723
- Yeah.
- <i>Right.</i>

748
00:32:43.757 --> 00:32:45.525
You got to remember

749
00:32:45.559 --> 00:32:47.360
- this is a jailhouse story.
- <i>All right.</i>

750
00:32:47.394 --> 00:32:49.829
- Which you specifically asked for.
- All right.

751
00:32:49.863 --> 00:32:51.831
The point is, Professor,

752
00:32:51.865 --> 00:32:53.900
I ain't got the first
notion in the world

753
00:32:53.934 --> 00:32:56.969
about what makes God tick.
I don't know why he spoke to me.

754
00:32:57.004 --> 00:32:59.105
- I know I wouldn't have.
- But you listened.

755
00:32:59.139 --> 00:33:01.874
- What choice would you have?
- I don't know.

756
00:33:01.909 --> 00:33:03.776
Not listen.

757
00:33:03.811 --> 00:33:07.613
You think he goes around
talking to people

758
00:33:07.648 --> 00:33:10.817
he knows ain't gonna listen
to him in the first place?

759
00:33:13.720 --> 00:33:17.056
You think he got
that kind of free time?

760
00:33:17.090 --> 00:33:19.992
Why is it you people
cat just accept it

761
00:33:20.027 --> 00:33:22.528
that some people don't even
want to believe in God?

762
00:33:22.563 --> 00:33:24.964
- I can accept that.
- You can?

763
00:33:24.998 --> 00:33:28.367
Sure I can.
Meaning I believe that to be a fact.

764
00:33:28.402 --> 00:33:30.837
I mean I'm looking
at it every day,

765
00:33:30.871 --> 00:33:32.472
so I'd better accept it.

766
00:33:32.506 --> 00:33:34.006
Then why can't you
leave us alone?

767
00:33:34.041 --> 00:33:36.876
Oh, to be hanging from
steampipes and all that?

768
00:33:36.910 --> 00:33:39.912
If that's what
we want to do, yes.

769
00:33:39.947 --> 00:33:44.584
Because he said not to.

770
00:33:47.421 --> 00:33:49.989
It's in here.

771
00:33:53.727 --> 00:33:56.362
- Don't you want to be happy?
- Happy?

772
00:33:56.396 --> 00:33:58.197
What, you got something
against being happy?

773
00:33:58.232 --> 00:34:00.766
- God help us.
- What, I done opened up a can of worms?

774
00:34:00.801 --> 00:34:02.268
What you got against
being happy?

775
00:34:02.302 --> 00:34:04.203
It's contrary
to the human condition.

776
00:34:04.238 --> 00:34:06.572
Contrary to <i>your</i> condition.
I gotta agree with that.

777
00:34:06.607 --> 00:34:07.907
Happy?

778
00:34:07.941 --> 00:34:10.309
What, like there ain't
no such thing?

779
00:34:10.344 --> 00:34:12.278
Not for nobody?

780
00:34:12.312 --> 00:34:13.379
No.

781
00:34:13.413 --> 00:34:15.414
How did we get ourselves
in such a fix as this?

782
00:34:15.449 --> 00:34:17.917
We were born
in such a fix as this.

783
00:34:17.951 --> 00:34:20.686
Suffering and human destiny
are the same thing.

784
00:34:20.721 --> 00:34:22.522
Each is a description
of the other.

785
00:34:22.556 --> 00:34:24.257
We ain't talking
about no suffering.

786
00:34:24.291 --> 00:34:25.892
We're talking
about being happy.

787
00:34:25.926 --> 00:34:28.094
- You can't be happy if you're in pain.
- Why not?

788
00:34:28.128 --> 00:34:29.428
You're not making any sense.

789
00:34:29.463 --> 00:34:32.632
Oh, them is some hard words
from the Professor.

790
00:34:32.666 --> 00:34:35.034
The preacher has fell back.

791
00:34:35.068 --> 00:34:36.669
He's clutching his heart.

792
00:34:36.703 --> 00:34:39.205
His eyes is rolled back
in his head.

793
00:34:39.239 --> 00:34:43.309
Wait a minute.
Wait a minute, folks.

794
00:34:43.343 --> 00:34:47.146
The preacher's blinking.
He's coming back.

795
00:34:47.180 --> 00:34:50.049
He's coming back.

796
00:34:50.083 --> 00:34:52.084
The point is, Professor,

797
00:34:52.119 --> 00:34:54.287
that if you ain't got
no pain in your life,

798
00:34:54.321 --> 00:34:56.822
how would you even know
when you was happy?

799
00:34:56.857 --> 00:34:58.124
As compared to what?

800
00:34:58.158 --> 00:35:00.426
You don't have anything to
drink around here, do you?

801
00:35:00.460 --> 00:35:02.929
No, I don't.

802
00:35:02.963 --> 00:35:04.864
Is you a drinking man?

803
00:35:04.898 --> 00:35:07.633
- Are we about to get a temperance lecture?
- Not from me.

804
00:35:07.668 --> 00:35:09.702
It's been a difficult day.

805
00:35:09.736 --> 00:35:11.537
I take it you don't drink.

806
00:35:11.572 --> 00:35:14.040
No, I don't.

807
00:35:14.074 --> 00:35:16.776
I've done my share of it
in my time though.

808
00:35:16.810 --> 00:35:18.077
Are you in A.A.?

809
00:35:18.111 --> 00:35:20.947
No no, no A.A.
I just quit.

810
00:35:20.981 --> 00:35:24.216
I've had a lot of friends
who was drinkers though...

811
00:35:24.251 --> 00:35:26.986
Most of them for that matter.
Most of them dead too.

812
00:35:27.020 --> 00:35:28.220
From drinking?

813
00:35:28.255 --> 00:35:31.123
Well, from drinking or reasons
not too far from it.

814
00:35:31.158 --> 00:35:34.860
I had a friend get run down not
too long ago by a taxicab.

815
00:35:34.895 --> 00:35:37.597
Now where you think
he was headed drunk?

816
00:35:37.631 --> 00:35:39.398
I don't know.
Where was he going?

817
00:35:39.433 --> 00:35:41.634
To get more whisky.

818
00:35:41.668 --> 00:35:43.502
He had plenty at the house,

819
00:35:43.537 --> 00:35:46.238
but drunks are always scared
they're gonna run out.

820
00:35:46.273 --> 00:35:47.740
<i>Was he killed?</i>

821
00:35:47.774 --> 00:35:49.542
I hope so.
We buried him.

822
00:35:49.576 --> 00:35:52.078
I suppose there's
a moral to this story.

823
00:35:54.247 --> 00:35:57.350
Just a story about what you
get and what you want.

824
00:35:57.384 --> 00:35:59.485
Pain... happiness.

825
00:36:03.223 --> 00:36:06.692
Let me tell you another one.

826
00:36:06.727 --> 00:36:08.694
One Sunday

827
00:36:08.729 --> 00:36:12.898
we's all sitting around the house
drinking... a Sunday morning.

828
00:36:12.933 --> 00:36:15.568
<i>Well, here come this friend of ours</i>
<i>with this girl Evelyn.</i>

829
00:36:15.602 --> 00:36:18.204
Now Evelyn was drunk
when she got there,

830
00:36:18.238 --> 00:36:20.172
but we told her,

831
00:36:20.207 --> 00:36:21.874
"Go on and get yourself
a drink."

832
00:36:21.908 --> 00:36:24.577
Well, directly, my buddy Reg...

833
00:36:24.611 --> 00:36:29.181
He goes in the kitchen
to fix hisself a drink.

834
00:36:29.216 --> 00:36:33.352
Only now the bottle is gone.

835
00:36:33.387 --> 00:36:36.322
Now Reg has been around a few
drinking people in his time

836
00:36:36.356 --> 00:36:38.024
and commences to hunt
for the bottle.

837
00:36:38.058 --> 00:36:40.393
He looks in the cabinet,
looks behind everything,

838
00:36:40.427 --> 00:36:41.961
but he can't find it.

839
00:36:41.995 --> 00:36:44.096
Of course he knows
what's happened to it.

840
00:36:44.131 --> 00:36:46.565
So he comes back in the
living room, sits down,

841
00:36:46.600 --> 00:36:50.169
looks at Evelyn sitting there
on the sofa drunk as a goat.

842
00:36:50.203 --> 00:36:53.839
And he says, "Evelyn,
where you put the whisky?"

843
00:36:53.874 --> 00:36:55.708
Evelyn says...

844
00:36:59.012 --> 00:37:00.946
He says, "Evelyn,

845
00:37:00.981 --> 00:37:03.783
where did you hide
the whisky?"

846
00:37:03.817 --> 00:37:04.950
And she...

847
00:37:09.389 --> 00:37:12.358
Well, Reg is sitting there and this is
beginning to piss him off a little,

848
00:37:12.392 --> 00:37:14.093
so he gets in her face
and he says,

849
00:37:18.432 --> 00:37:19.765
And she says,

850
00:37:19.800 --> 00:37:21.801
"I hid it in the toilet."

851
00:37:29.276 --> 00:37:30.409
That's pretty funny.

852
00:37:30.444 --> 00:37:32.044
Oh, I thought you'd like that.

853
00:37:32.079 --> 00:37:33.779
And is that where
the whisky was?

854
00:37:33.814 --> 00:37:35.481
Oh, yeah yeah.
That's a favorite place

855
00:37:35.515 --> 00:37:37.383
for drunks to hide a bottle.

856
00:37:37.417 --> 00:37:40.386
But the point is
that the drunk's concern

857
00:37:40.420 --> 00:37:43.689
ain't that he's going to die from drinking...
which he is...

858
00:37:43.724 --> 00:37:46.092
but that he's going to
run out of whisky

859
00:37:46.126 --> 00:37:48.160
before he get
a chance to do it.

860
00:37:48.195 --> 00:37:49.862
Is you hungry?

861
00:37:49.896 --> 00:37:52.098
'Cause I can come back to this.
I ain't gonna lose my place.

862
00:37:52.132 --> 00:37:53.632
No, I'm all right.
Go ahead.

863
00:37:53.667 --> 00:37:55.901
If you was to hand
a drunk a drink

864
00:37:55.936 --> 00:37:58.270
and tell him he don't want it,
what do you think he'd say?

865
00:37:58.305 --> 00:37:59.939
I think I know
what he would say.

866
00:37:59.973 --> 00:38:01.574
Sure you do.
And you would be right.

867
00:38:01.608 --> 00:38:03.209
- About him not really wanting it?
- Yes.

868
00:38:03.243 --> 00:38:06.045
Because what he wants he can't
get, or thinks he can't get,

869
00:38:06.079 --> 00:38:09.048
so what he really don't want
he can't get enough of.

870
00:38:09.082 --> 00:38:10.916
So what is it he really wants?

871
00:38:10.951 --> 00:38:13.285
Well, you know
what he really wants.

872
00:38:13.320 --> 00:38:14.520
- No, I don't.
- Yes, you do.

873
00:38:14.554 --> 00:38:15.921
No, I don't.

874
00:38:15.956 --> 00:38:17.923
You don't know what he wants?

875
00:38:17.958 --> 00:38:19.625
No, I do not.

876
00:38:19.659 --> 00:38:22.161
He wants what everybody wants.

877
00:38:22.195 --> 00:38:23.295
And that is?

878
00:38:23.330 --> 00:38:24.997
He wants to be loved by God.

879
00:38:25.031 --> 00:38:26.866
I don't want
to be loved by God.

880
00:38:26.900 --> 00:38:29.268
See, I love that.
I love the way you cut right to it.

881
00:38:29.302 --> 00:38:31.437
See, he don't either,
according to him.

882
00:38:31.471 --> 00:38:34.240
All he wants
is a drink of whisky.

883
00:38:34.274 --> 00:38:36.008
Now you're a smart man,
Professor.

884
00:38:36.042 --> 00:38:38.077
Now tell me which one makes
sense and which one don't.

885
00:38:38.111 --> 00:38:39.745
I don't want a drink
of whisky either.

886
00:38:39.780 --> 00:38:41.347
I thought you just got
done asking for one.

887
00:38:41.381 --> 00:38:43.149
I mean as a general
proposition.

888
00:38:43.183 --> 00:38:45.818
We ain't talking about no general proposition.
We're talking about a drink.

889
00:38:45.852 --> 00:38:48.387
- I don't have a drinking problem.
- You got some kind of problem.

890
00:38:48.421 --> 00:38:50.890
Well, whatever kind of problem
I have, it is not something

891
00:38:50.924 --> 00:38:53.526
that I imagine can be addressed
with a drink of liquor.

892
00:38:53.560 --> 00:38:57.263
Mm! I love the way
you put that.

893
00:38:57.297 --> 00:38:59.265
Well, what can it be
addressed with?

894
00:38:59.299 --> 00:39:02.368
I think you know what
it can be addressed with.

895
00:39:02.402 --> 00:39:04.336
The Sunset Limited.

896
00:39:04.371 --> 00:39:06.338
Yes.

897
00:39:06.373 --> 00:39:08.307
That's what you really want.

898
00:39:08.341 --> 00:39:10.843
That is what I want, yes.

899
00:39:12.379 --> 00:39:14.780
Well, that's a mighty big
drink of whisky, Professor.

900
00:39:14.815 --> 00:39:17.383
- That I don't really want?
- That you don't really want, yes.

901
00:39:17.417 --> 00:39:20.252
- Well, I think I do want it.
- Of course you do, honey.

902
00:39:20.287 --> 00:39:21.921
If you didn't, we
wouldn't be sitting here.

903
00:39:21.955 --> 00:39:23.455
I disagree with you.

904
00:39:23.490 --> 00:39:25.457
All right, but that's
the hand I'm playing.

905
00:39:25.492 --> 00:39:27.760
I don't think you understand
that people such as myself

906
00:39:27.794 --> 00:39:30.729
look upon this yearning for God as
something lacking in these people.

907
00:39:30.764 --> 00:39:33.199
Sure I do.
I couldn't agree more.

908
00:39:33.233 --> 00:39:34.333
You agree with that?

909
00:39:34.367 --> 00:39:37.970
Yes.
What's lacking is God.

910
00:39:38.004 --> 00:39:41.173
I am sorry, but to me this whole
idea of God is just a load of crap.

911
00:39:41.208 --> 00:39:43.843
Oh lord have mercy!

912
00:39:43.877 --> 00:39:45.511
Jesus help us.

913
00:39:45.545 --> 00:39:48.581
The Professor's done gone
and blasphemed all over us.

914
00:39:48.615 --> 00:39:51.250
We ain't never
gonna be saved now.

915
00:39:53.119 --> 00:39:55.020
You don't find that
an evil thing to say?

916
00:39:55.055 --> 00:39:57.923
No, Professor, I don't.
But you does.

917
00:39:57.958 --> 00:39:59.625
No, I don't.
It's simply a fact.

918
00:39:59.659 --> 00:40:01.193
No, it ain't simply no fact.

919
00:40:01.228 --> 00:40:02.795
Now that's the biggest
fact about you.

920
00:40:02.829 --> 00:40:05.030
In fact, it might just
be the only fact.

921
00:40:05.065 --> 00:40:06.999
But you don't seem
to think it's so bad.

922
00:40:07.033 --> 00:40:09.435
No, 'cause I know it
to be curable.

923
00:40:09.469 --> 00:40:11.437
Now if you're asking me

924
00:40:11.471 --> 00:40:14.106
what the man up there
thinks about it,

925
00:40:14.140 --> 00:40:17.409
well, I imagine
he done heard it so much

926
00:40:17.444 --> 00:40:19.945
that it don't bother him
all that much.

927
00:40:19.980 --> 00:40:21.947
I mean,

928
00:40:21.982 --> 00:40:24.817
what if somebody told you
you didn't exist

929
00:40:24.851 --> 00:40:26.485
and you was sitting right there

930
00:40:26.519 --> 00:40:28.821
listening to him say it?

931
00:40:28.855 --> 00:40:30.823
That wouldn't piss you off,
would it?

932
00:40:30.857 --> 00:40:32.725
No, you'd just
feel sorry for them.

933
00:40:32.759 --> 00:40:34.860
Right, and you might even try
to get some help for them.

934
00:40:34.895 --> 00:40:38.297
Now in my case
he had to holler out loud,

935
00:40:38.331 --> 00:40:41.467
me laying on a slab
in two pieces,

936
00:40:41.501 --> 00:40:43.569
sewed back together
where some nigger done tried

937
00:40:43.603 --> 00:40:45.304
to core me like an apple.

938
00:40:45.338 --> 00:40:49.308
But God being God,

939
00:40:49.342 --> 00:40:52.411
he can speak to your heart
at any time.

940
00:40:52.445 --> 00:40:55.114
And furthermore,
if he spoke to me...

941
00:40:55.148 --> 00:40:57.082
which he did...

942
00:40:57.117 --> 00:40:59.952
he can speak to anybody.

943
00:41:16.636 --> 00:41:19.104
"Wonder what this
crazy nigger's

944
00:41:19.139 --> 00:41:21.507
fixing to do now.

945
00:41:21.541 --> 00:41:23.909
He's liable to put
the mojo on me.

946
00:41:23.944 --> 00:41:27.579
He'll be speaking in tongues
here directly.

947
00:41:27.614 --> 00:41:30.783
I need to get my ass
out of here.

948
00:41:30.817 --> 00:41:33.485
He's liable to steal
my pocketbook.

949
00:41:33.520 --> 00:41:37.222
I need to get my ass
on down to that depot

950
00:41:37.257 --> 00:41:40.125
'fore something bad
happens to me."

951
00:41:41.528 --> 00:41:44.730
Hey!

952
00:41:44.764 --> 00:41:47.399
What are we gonna do
with you, Professor?

953
00:41:47.434 --> 00:41:49.835
Look, I know I owe you
a great deal,

954
00:41:49.869 --> 00:41:52.171
at least in the eyes
of the world.

955
00:41:52.205 --> 00:41:55.107
Couldn't I just give you something
and we could call it square?

956
00:41:56.543 --> 00:41:59.144
I'll give you some money,
something like that.

957
00:41:59.179 --> 00:42:01.347
I can give you $1000.

958
00:42:01.381 --> 00:42:02.881
Well, that's not
very much, I guess.

959
00:42:02.916 --> 00:42:04.483
I can give you $3000, say.

960
00:42:04.517 --> 00:42:06.919
You ain't got no notion of the
trouble you're in, do you?

961
00:42:06.953 --> 00:42:08.921
- I don't know what you mean.
- I know you don't.

962
00:42:08.955 --> 00:42:11.256
I'd just like to
settle this in some way.

963
00:42:11.291 --> 00:42:13.292
Ain't me you got
to settle with.

964
00:42:13.326 --> 00:42:16.295
Do you really think
I was sent to you by God?

965
00:42:16.329 --> 00:42:18.630
No, it's worse than that.

966
00:42:19.566 --> 00:42:21.333
How do you mean?

967
00:42:21.368 --> 00:42:26.171
Belief ain't like unbelief.

968
00:42:26.206 --> 00:42:28.140
If you're a believer

969
00:42:28.174 --> 00:42:31.744
and you finally got to come
to the well of belief itself,

970
00:42:31.778 --> 00:42:35.047
then you ain't got to look no further.
There ain't no further.

971
00:42:35.081 --> 00:42:37.182
But the unbeliever's
got a problem.

972
00:42:37.217 --> 00:42:39.218
He's set out
to unravel the world.

973
00:42:39.252 --> 00:42:41.587
For everything he can
point to that ain't true,

974
00:42:41.621 --> 00:42:43.589
he leaves two things
laying there.

975
00:42:53.636 --> 00:42:56.872
Do you really believe everything
that's in there... the Bible?

976
00:42:57.925 --> 00:42:58.793
The literal truth?

977
00:42:58.838 --> 00:43:00.139
<i>Yes.</i>

978
00:43:00.173 --> 00:43:02.141
Probably not,

979
00:43:02.175 --> 00:43:04.343
but then you already know
I'm an outlaw.

980
00:43:04.377 --> 00:43:08.280
What is it you would
disagree with?

981
00:43:08.315 --> 00:43:10.349
Probably the notion
of original sin...

982
00:43:10.383 --> 00:43:12.785
you know, when Eve
ate the apple,

983
00:43:12.819 --> 00:43:14.753
turned everybody bad.

984
00:43:14.788 --> 00:43:16.755
I don't see people that way.

985
00:43:16.790 --> 00:43:18.757
I think for the most part

986
00:43:18.792 --> 00:43:20.859
people was good to begin with.

987
00:43:20.894 --> 00:43:24.063
I think evil is something
you bring on your own self,

988
00:43:24.097 --> 00:43:26.298
mostly from wanting things
you ain't supposed to have.

989
00:43:26.333 --> 00:43:28.834
But I ain't gonna stand here and
tell you about me being a heretic

990
00:43:28.868 --> 00:43:30.869
when I'm trying to get you
to quit being one.

991
00:43:30.904 --> 00:43:32.838
Are you a heretic?

992
00:43:32.872 --> 00:43:35.674
Is you trying to put me
in the trick bag, Professor?

993
00:43:35.709 --> 00:43:37.343
No, I'm not.

994
00:43:37.377 --> 00:43:38.610
Are you?

995
00:43:42.982 --> 00:43:46.118
No more than
what a man ought to be,

996
00:43:46.152 --> 00:43:50.155
even a man
with a powerful belief.

997
00:43:50.190 --> 00:43:53.826
I ain't a doubter,
but I am a questioner.

998
00:43:53.860 --> 00:43:55.661
What's the difference?

999
00:43:55.695 --> 00:43:59.365
A questioner wants the truth.

1000
00:43:59.399 --> 00:44:03.235
A doubter wants to be told
there ain't no such thing.

1001
00:44:03.269 --> 00:44:05.971
You don't think you have to believe
everything that's in there

1002
00:44:06.005 --> 00:44:08.073
- in order to be saved?
- No, not at all.

1003
00:44:08.108 --> 00:44:10.309
I don't even think
you have to read it.

1004
00:44:10.343 --> 00:44:12.311
I ain't even for sure
you even got to know

1005
00:44:12.345 --> 00:44:13.812
there is such a book.

1006
00:44:13.847 --> 00:44:16.815
I think whatever truth is wrote
in the pages of this book

1007
00:44:16.850 --> 00:44:19.485
is wrote in the human heart too, and
was wrote there a long time ago,

1008
00:44:19.519 --> 00:44:21.820
and will be wrote there
a long time hence,

1009
00:44:21.855 --> 00:44:25.023
even if every copy of this book is burned...
every copy of it.

1010
00:44:25.058 --> 00:44:28.260
And what Jesus said...
I don't think he made up a word of it.

1011
00:44:28.294 --> 00:44:29.828
He just told it.

1012
00:44:29.863 --> 00:44:31.730
This book is a guide

1013
00:44:31.765 --> 00:44:33.932
for the ignorant
and the sick at heart.

1014
00:44:33.967 --> 00:44:35.801
A whole man
wouldn't need it at all.

1015
00:44:35.835 --> 00:44:37.636
And if you was
to read this book,

1016
00:44:37.670 --> 00:44:40.873
you're gonna find there's more
talk in here about the wrong way

1017
00:44:40.907 --> 00:44:43.275
than there is the right way.
Why is that?

1018
00:44:43.309 --> 00:44:44.676
I don't know.
Why is it?

1019
00:44:44.711 --> 00:44:46.078
Why don't you tell me?

1020
00:44:46.112 --> 00:44:48.547
I'll have to think about that.

1021
00:44:48.581 --> 00:44:49.681
- Okay.
- Okay what?

1022
00:44:49.716 --> 00:44:51.417
Okay, go on and think about it.

1023
00:44:51.451 --> 00:44:54.253
It might take me a little longer
than you to think about something.

1024
00:44:54.287 --> 00:44:56.655
- That's all right.
- That's all right?

1025
00:44:56.689 --> 00:44:59.591
Yeah. I mean, I can take that
statement in one or two ways.

1026
00:44:59.626 --> 00:45:01.894
I'm going to take it the good
way 'cause that's my nature.

1027
00:45:01.928 --> 00:45:03.929
That way I get to live
in my world and not yours.

1028
00:45:03.963 --> 00:45:05.731
What makes you think
mine is so bad?

1029
00:45:05.765 --> 00:45:07.232
I don't think it's so bad.

1030
00:45:07.267 --> 00:45:08.834
I just know it's brief.

1031
00:45:08.868 --> 00:45:10.602
All right.

1032
00:45:10.637 --> 00:45:13.105
I think the answer
to your question is

1033
00:45:13.139 --> 00:45:14.940
that the dialectic
of the homily

1034
00:45:14.974 --> 00:45:17.075
always presupposes
a ground of evil.

1035
00:45:17.110 --> 00:45:19.077
- Man.
- How's that?

1036
00:45:19.112 --> 00:45:21.613
That's strong as a mare's
breath, Professor.

1037
00:45:21.648 --> 00:45:23.115
Whoo! Wouldn't I love

1038
00:45:23.149 --> 00:45:25.784
to lay some shit like that
on the brothers. Whoa.

1039
00:45:25.819 --> 00:45:29.788
It's just the two of us sitting
here in private, talking.

1040
00:45:29.823 --> 00:45:32.057
What'd you just say?

1041
00:45:32.091 --> 00:45:34.026
Your question...

1042
00:45:34.060 --> 00:45:37.129
the Bible is full
of cautionary tales...

1043
00:45:37.163 --> 00:45:38.864
all of literature,
for that matter...

1044
00:45:38.898 --> 00:45:41.633
telling us to be careful.
Careful of what?

1045
00:45:41.668 --> 00:45:43.635
Taking the wrong turn,
the wrong path.

1046
00:45:43.670 --> 00:45:45.537
How many wrong paths are there?

1047
00:45:45.572 --> 00:45:47.539
Their number is legion.

1048
00:45:47.574 --> 00:45:49.708
How many right paths?
Just one.

1049
00:45:49.742 --> 00:45:53.378
Hence the imbalance
that you spoke of.

1050
00:45:53.413 --> 00:45:55.414
Man.

1051
00:45:55.448 --> 00:45:57.549
You know, you could go
on TV, Professor,

1052
00:45:57.584 --> 00:45:59.451
a good-looking man like yourself.
You know that?

1053
00:45:59.486 --> 00:46:01.386
- Stop.
- I'm serious.

1054
00:46:01.421 --> 00:46:04.957
I wasn't even sure you was a Professor
till you laid that shit on me.

1055
00:46:04.991 --> 00:46:06.892
Now I think you're having
fun at my expense.

1056
00:46:06.926 --> 00:46:08.694
- No, I ain't.
- Well, I think you are.

1057
00:46:08.728 --> 00:46:11.129
Honey, I swear I ain't.

1058
00:46:11.164 --> 00:46:14.433
I couldn't say a thing like
you just got done saying.

1059
00:46:14.467 --> 00:46:15.567
I admire that.

1060
00:46:15.602 --> 00:46:17.236
And why do you keep
calling me honey?

1061
00:46:17.270 --> 00:46:20.172
That's just the old South talking.
It don't mean nothing.

1062
00:46:20.206 --> 00:46:21.607
I'll try and stop
if it bothers you.

1063
00:46:21.641 --> 00:46:22.841
I'm just not sure
what it means.

1064
00:46:22.876 --> 00:46:24.743
It means you're among friends.

1065
00:46:24.777 --> 00:46:26.645
It means quit worrying
about everything.

1066
00:46:26.679 --> 00:46:28.747
That might be easier said than done.

1067
00:46:28.781 --> 00:46:30.716
Yeah, you're right.
It might.

1068
00:46:30.750 --> 00:46:33.218
But we're just talking...
talking.

1069
00:46:36.322 --> 00:46:38.390
- Tell me something.
- Sure.

1070
00:46:38.424 --> 00:46:41.026
Why are you here?
What do you get out of this?

1071
00:46:41.060 --> 00:46:42.461
You seem like a smart man.

1072
00:46:42.495 --> 00:46:44.363
Me?

1073
00:46:44.397 --> 00:46:46.932
I'm just a dumb country nigger
from Louisiana.

1074
00:46:46.966 --> 00:46:49.434
I done told you, I ain't never had
the first thought in my head.

1075
00:46:49.469 --> 00:46:53.038
If it ain't in here,
I don't know it.

1076
00:46:53.072 --> 00:46:55.641
Sometimes I think
you're having fun with me.

1077
00:46:57.143 --> 00:47:00.979
<i>You are an asshole!</i>
<i>A-s-s-hole! Asshole!</i>

1078
00:47:01.014 --> 00:47:02.648
<i>Come on now.</i>

1079
00:47:02.682 --> 00:47:05.450
<i>Now what the fuck are you doing</i>
<i>in my motherfucking bed?</i>

1080
00:47:08.788 --> 00:47:10.422
I don't see
how you can live here.

1081
00:47:10.456 --> 00:47:11.857
I don't know how
you can feel safe.

1082
00:47:11.891 --> 00:47:14.593
Well, you got a point, Professor,
about being safe anyway.

1083
00:47:14.627 --> 00:47:17.062
<i>You ever stopped any of these people</i>
<i>from taking drugs?</i>

1084
00:47:17.096 --> 00:47:19.097
Not that I know of.

1085
00:47:19.132 --> 00:47:21.366
Then what is the point?
I don't get it.

1086
00:47:21.401 --> 00:47:24.403
I mean, it's hopeless.
This places is a moral leper colony.

1087
00:47:24.437 --> 00:47:26.605
Damn.

1088
00:47:26.639 --> 00:47:29.841
Moral leper colony.
Where's my pencil at?

1089
00:47:29.876 --> 00:47:32.811
I ain't never gonna want you to leave.
Put this in my book.

1090
00:47:32.845 --> 00:47:35.847
"In the moral
leper colony."

1091
00:47:35.882 --> 00:47:37.349
I like the sound of that.

1092
00:47:39.886 --> 00:47:41.820
I still don't see
how you can live here.

1093
00:47:41.854 --> 00:47:45.591
Why not go someplace where you
can do the world some good?

1094
00:47:45.625 --> 00:47:48.460
As opposed to someplace
where good is needed?

1095
00:47:48.494 --> 00:47:50.329
Even God gives up
at some point.

1096
00:47:50.363 --> 00:47:52.197
There's no ministry in
hell that I ever heard of.

1097
00:47:52.231 --> 00:47:55.000
No, there ain't.
And that's well put.

1098
00:47:55.034 --> 00:47:57.169
Ministry is for the living.

1099
00:47:57.203 --> 00:47:59.438
That's why you're responsible
for your brother.

1100
00:47:59.472 --> 00:48:01.406
Once he quit breathing
you can't help him no more.

1101
00:48:01.441 --> 00:48:02.908
He's in the hands
of other parties.

1102
00:48:02.942 --> 00:48:04.876
So you got to
look after him now.

1103
00:48:04.911 --> 00:48:08.080
You might even want to
monitor his train schedule.

1104
00:48:08.114 --> 00:48:09.781
You think you are
your brother's keeper?

1105
00:48:09.816 --> 00:48:11.850
<i>I don't believe "think"</i>
<i>quite covers it.</i>

1106
00:48:11.884 --> 00:48:13.385
And Jesus is part
of this enterprise?

1107
00:48:13.419 --> 00:48:15.320
That all right with you?

1108
00:48:15.355 --> 00:48:17.756
And he's interested in
coming here to this cesspool

1109
00:48:17.790 --> 00:48:20.459
and salvaging what everybody
knows is unsalvageable.

1110
00:48:20.493 --> 00:48:23.495
Why would he do that?
You said he didn't have a lot of free time.

1111
00:48:23.529 --> 00:48:25.397
<i>Why would he</i>
<i>come here?</i>

1112
00:48:25.431 --> 00:48:28.133
What would the difference
be to him

1113
00:48:28.167 --> 00:48:30.769
between a building that was
spiritually and morally vacant

1114
00:48:30.803 --> 00:48:32.371
and one that was
just plain empty?

1115
00:48:32.405 --> 00:48:36.475
Mm, Professor, you're a theologian
here and I ain't even know it.

1116
00:48:36.509 --> 00:48:38.877
You're being facetious.

1117
00:48:38.911 --> 00:48:40.846
I don't know that word.

1118
00:48:40.880 --> 00:48:42.614
But don't be afraid
to talk down to me.

1119
00:48:42.649 --> 00:48:44.416
You ain't gonna
hurt my feelings.

1120
00:48:44.450 --> 00:48:46.084
It means...
I guess it means

1121
00:48:46.119 --> 00:48:47.953
you're not being sincere,
that you're being...

1122
00:48:47.987 --> 00:48:50.989
You don't mean what you're saying
in a cynical sort of way.

1123
00:48:51.024 --> 00:48:52.758
Mm, you don't think
I mean what I say?

1124
00:48:52.792 --> 00:48:55.327
I think you say things
for effect sometimes.

1125
00:48:55.361 --> 00:48:58.163
Mm, well, let me
say this for effect.

1126
00:48:58.197 --> 00:48:59.531
Go ahead.

1127
00:48:59.565 --> 00:49:01.500
Suppose I was to tell you

1128
00:49:01.534 --> 00:49:04.736
that if you could bring
yourself to unlatch your hands

1129
00:49:04.771 --> 00:49:06.505
from around your
brother's throat,

1130
00:49:06.539 --> 00:49:09.141
you could have
life everlasting?

1131
00:49:09.175 --> 00:49:11.043
There's no such thing.
Everybody dies.

1132
00:49:11.077 --> 00:49:12.544
That ain't what he said.

1133
00:49:12.578 --> 00:49:17.349
He said you could have
life everlasting,

1134
00:49:17.383 --> 00:49:20.352
have it now, today,

1135
00:49:20.386 --> 00:49:23.255
hold it in your hands,

1136
00:49:23.289 --> 00:49:25.691
see it.
It gives off a light,

1137
00:49:25.725 --> 00:49:30.295
got a little weight to it...
not much.

1138
00:49:30.329 --> 00:49:34.566
Warm to the touch
just a little...

1139
00:49:34.600 --> 00:49:38.570
Life, and it's forever.

1140
00:49:38.604 --> 00:49:41.039
And you can have it

1141
00:49:41.074 --> 00:49:44.176
now, today.

1142
00:49:44.210 --> 00:49:45.944
But you don't want it.

1143
00:49:45.978 --> 00:49:48.246
You don't want it
'cause to get it

1144
00:49:48.281 --> 00:49:50.315
you got to let your
brother off the hook.

1145
00:49:50.349 --> 00:49:54.519
You got to actually take him
in your arms and hold him

1146
00:49:54.554 --> 00:49:57.322
and don't matter what color
he is or what he smells like

1147
00:49:57.356 --> 00:49:59.291
or even if he don't
want to be held.

1148
00:49:59.325 --> 00:50:01.359
And you won't do it

1149
00:50:01.394 --> 00:50:03.562
'cause you don't think
he deserves it.

1150
00:50:03.596 --> 00:50:05.397
And about that
there ain't no argument.

1151
00:50:05.431 --> 00:50:07.566
He don't.

1152
00:50:07.600 --> 00:50:09.601
You won't do it
'cause it ain't just.

1153
00:50:09.635 --> 00:50:11.603
Ain't that so?

1154
00:50:11.637 --> 00:50:14.039
Ain't it?

1155
00:50:14.073 --> 00:50:15.741
I don't think in those terms.

1156
00:50:15.775 --> 00:50:19.144
Just answer
the question, Professor.

1157
00:50:19.178 --> 00:50:20.979
I don't believe
in that sort of thing.

1158
00:50:21.013 --> 00:50:24.883
I know you don't.
Just answer the question.

1159
00:50:24.917 --> 00:50:28.887
There may be some truth
in what you say.

1160
00:50:28.921 --> 00:50:30.622
That's all I'm going to get?

1161
00:50:30.656 --> 00:50:31.823
Yes.

1162
00:50:31.858 --> 00:50:33.759
All right, all right.
I'll take it.

1163
00:50:33.793 --> 00:50:36.561
Some is a lot.
We down to breadcrumbs here.

1164
00:50:36.596 --> 00:50:37.796
I really have to go home.

1165
00:50:37.830 --> 00:50:40.065
No no no, just stay,
just a little.

1166
00:50:40.099 --> 00:50:42.134
We can talk about
something else.

1167
00:50:42.168 --> 00:50:44.002
You like baseball?

1168
00:50:45.438 --> 00:50:47.973
Tell you what...
Why don't I fix us something to eat?

1169
00:50:48.007 --> 00:50:50.709
- I'm not hungry.
- How about some coffee?

1170
00:50:50.743 --> 00:50:52.511
All right.

1171
00:50:52.545 --> 00:50:54.246
But then I really have to go.

1172
00:50:54.280 --> 00:50:57.382
All right.
The man says all right.

1173
00:51:03.823 --> 00:51:07.826
You know, ordinarily
I wouldn't be this rude...

1174
00:51:07.860 --> 00:51:10.328
A man come in my house,
sit at my table

1175
00:51:10.363 --> 00:51:12.330
and me not offering nothing.

1176
00:51:12.365 --> 00:51:15.801
But with you
I got to strategize,

1177
00:51:15.835 --> 00:51:17.836
<i>play the cards right,</i>

1178
00:51:17.870 --> 00:51:19.938
<i>keep you from slipping</i>
<i>off into the night.</i>

1179
00:51:19.972 --> 00:51:21.940
It's not night.

1180
00:51:21.974 --> 00:51:24.242
Depends on what kind of
night you're talking about.

1181
00:51:24.277 --> 00:51:26.444
Let me ask you a kind of
personal question.

1182
00:51:26.479 --> 00:51:28.780
This will be good.

1183
00:51:28.815 --> 00:51:32.050
What you think
is wrong with you

1184
00:51:32.084 --> 00:51:35.420
that you finally narrowed all your
choices down to the Sunset Limited?

1185
00:51:35.454 --> 00:51:37.289
I don't think there's
anything wrong with me.

1186
00:51:37.323 --> 00:51:39.658
I think I've just been driven
to finally face the truth.

1187
00:51:39.692 --> 00:51:41.426
If I'm different it
doesn't mean I'm crazy.

1188
00:51:41.460 --> 00:51:42.527
- Different?
- Yeah.

1189
00:51:42.562 --> 00:51:44.162
- Different from who?
- Anybody.

1190
00:51:44.197 --> 00:51:46.264
What about all them other folk out
there trying to off themself?

1191
00:51:46.299 --> 00:51:48.433
- What about 'em?
- Well, maybe them's the folk you is like.

1192
00:51:48.467 --> 00:51:51.369
Maybe they're your natural kin, only
y'all don't get together all that much.

1193
00:51:51.404 --> 00:51:53.004
- I don't think so.
- Don't think so?

1194
00:51:53.039 --> 00:51:55.307
No, I've been in group
therapy with those people.

1195
00:51:55.341 --> 00:51:57.909
I've never found anyone there
that I felt any kinship with.

1196
00:51:57.944 --> 00:52:00.946
Hmm, what about all them professors?
There ain't no kinship there?

1197
00:52:00.980 --> 00:52:03.682
- Good God.
- I'll take that as a no.

1198
00:52:03.716 --> 00:52:05.617
I loathe them
and they loathe me.

1199
00:52:05.651 --> 00:52:08.320
Well, just 'cause
you don't like 'em

1200
00:52:08.354 --> 00:52:10.522
don't mean you ain't like 'em.

1201
00:52:10.556 --> 00:52:12.224
And what's that word "loathe"?

1202
00:52:12.258 --> 00:52:14.693
- Loathe.
- That a pretty powerful word, ain't it?

1203
00:52:14.727 --> 00:52:16.127
Not powerful enough,
I'm afraid.

1204
00:52:16.162 --> 00:52:18.263
How come you be loathing
these other professors?

1205
00:52:18.297 --> 00:52:20.432
- I know what you're thinking.
- What am I thinking?

1206
00:52:20.466 --> 00:52:23.068
You're thinking that I loathe
them because I'm like them,

1207
00:52:23.102 --> 00:52:24.502
and I loathe myself.

1208
00:52:24.537 --> 00:52:26.972
Let me ask you this...

1209
00:52:27.006 --> 00:52:28.974
Is you on any kind
of medication?

1210
00:52:29.008 --> 00:52:30.775
- No.
- There ain't no medication

1211
00:52:30.810 --> 00:52:32.611
for pilgrims out there
waiting to take the Limited?

1212
00:52:32.645 --> 00:52:34.579
- For suicidal depression?
- Yeah.

1213
00:52:34.614 --> 00:52:36.748
- Yeah, there is. I've tried them.
- And what happened?

1214
00:52:36.782 --> 00:52:38.316
- Nothing happened.
- You ain't get no relief?

1215
00:52:38.351 --> 00:52:40.852
- No. Your coffee's percolated.
- Yeah, I know.

1216
00:52:40.887 --> 00:52:43.722
Does these drugs work
for other people?

1217
00:52:43.756 --> 00:52:45.690
Yes, for most.

1218
00:52:45.725 --> 00:52:47.859
- But not for you?
- Not for me, no.

1219
00:52:47.894 --> 00:52:50.028
Hmm, what you make of that?

1220
00:52:50.062 --> 00:52:51.796
I don't know.
What am I supposed to make of it?

1221
00:52:51.831 --> 00:52:53.798
I don't know, Professor.
I'm just trying to see

1222
00:52:53.833 --> 00:52:56.167
if we can find you some
constituents out here.

1223
00:52:56.202 --> 00:52:58.236
- Constituents?
- Yeah, you like that?

1224
00:52:58.271 --> 00:53:00.238
Is that a word they
use on the streets?

1225
00:53:00.273 --> 00:53:02.641
No, I learned that word
in the jailhouse.

1226
00:53:02.675 --> 00:53:05.377
You hear things from
these jailhouse lawyers

1227
00:53:05.411 --> 00:53:07.379
and then it gets used around.

1228
00:53:07.413 --> 00:53:09.915
Be talking about
your constituents,

1229
00:53:09.949 --> 00:53:12.717
some other cat's constituents,

1230
00:53:12.752 --> 00:53:14.986
your wife's constituents.

1231
00:53:15.021 --> 00:53:16.888
You take cream and sugar?

1232
00:53:16.923 --> 00:53:18.256
No, just black.

1233
00:53:18.291 --> 00:53:19.791
Just black.

1234
00:53:23.696 --> 00:53:26.498
Why do I have to
have constituents?

1235
00:53:28.701 --> 00:53:30.702
I ain't saying
you got to, Professor.

1236
00:53:30.736 --> 00:53:32.270
I'm just wondering
if maybe you do

1237
00:53:32.305 --> 00:53:34.139
and we just ain't looked
hard enough for them.

1238
00:53:34.173 --> 00:53:36.841
They could be out there.
There could be some other

1239
00:53:36.876 --> 00:53:39.210
drug-proof terminal commuters out
there could be your friends.

1240
00:53:39.245 --> 00:53:41.446
- Terminal commuters?
- Mm-hmm, you like the sound of that?

1241
00:53:41.480 --> 00:53:43.782
It's all right.

1242
00:53:43.816 --> 00:53:45.050
Nobody?

1243
00:53:45.084 --> 00:53:47.152
- Nobody, no.
- Hmm.

1244
00:53:47.186 --> 00:53:49.554
I'm not a member.
Never wanted to be and never was.

1245
00:53:49.588 --> 00:53:52.457
- Not a member?
- No.

1246
00:53:52.491 --> 00:53:54.492
Look, I could eat a bite.

1247
00:53:54.527 --> 00:53:56.761
And I think you could too.

1248
00:54:00.566 --> 00:54:03.201
You break bread with a man,

1249
00:54:03.235 --> 00:54:05.870
you've moved on to another
level of friendship.

1250
00:54:05.905 --> 00:54:08.606
I heard that's true
the world over.

1251
00:54:08.641 --> 00:54:09.874
Probably.

1252
00:54:09.909 --> 00:54:11.409
Probably?

1253
00:54:11.444 --> 00:54:13.144
I like probably.

1254
00:54:13.179 --> 00:54:15.146
Probably from you is worth

1255
00:54:15.181 --> 00:54:16.982
a couple of damn rights
anywhere else.

1256
00:54:17.016 --> 00:54:19.117
Why? Because I don't
believe in anything?

1257
00:54:19.151 --> 00:54:23.088
Well, I don't think
that's the problem.

1258
00:54:23.122 --> 00:54:25.991
I think it's what
you do believe

1259
00:54:26.025 --> 00:54:27.525
that's carrying you off,

1260
00:54:27.560 --> 00:54:30.562
not what you don't.

1261
00:54:30.596 --> 00:54:32.564
Let me ask you this.

1262
00:54:32.598 --> 00:54:34.232
Go ahead.

1263
00:54:34.266 --> 00:54:36.201
You ever think about Jesus?

1264
00:54:36.235 --> 00:54:37.335
Here we go.

1265
00:54:37.370 --> 00:54:39.404
<i>Well, do you?</i>

1266
00:54:39.438 --> 00:54:42.640
What makes you think I'm not Jewish?

1267
00:54:42.675 --> 00:54:44.976
What, Jews ain't allowed
to think about Jesus?

1268
00:54:45.011 --> 00:54:47.212
No, but they might think
about him differently.

1269
00:54:47.246 --> 00:54:48.480
Oh.

1270
00:54:48.514 --> 00:54:49.981
Is you Jewish?

1271
00:54:50.016 --> 00:54:51.549
No, as it happens I'm not.

1272
00:54:51.584 --> 00:54:53.718
Had me going there
for a minute.

1273
00:54:53.753 --> 00:54:55.620
What, you don't like Jews?

1274
00:54:55.654 --> 00:54:58.056
<i>I'm just pulling</i>
<i>your chain, Professor,</i>

1275
00:54:58.090 --> 00:55:00.058
just pulling your chain.

1276
00:55:00.092 --> 00:55:03.128
I don't know why
I like to mess with you,

1277
00:55:03.162 --> 00:55:06.097
but I do.

1278
00:55:06.132 --> 00:55:08.566
Now you need to listen

1279
00:55:08.601 --> 00:55:10.502
or you need to believe
what you're hearing,

1280
00:55:10.536 --> 00:55:12.370
<i>'cause the point</i>
<i>of where this is going...</i>

1281
00:55:12.405 --> 00:55:14.239
which you wanted to know...

1282
00:55:14.273 --> 00:55:17.175
is that there ain't no Jews,
there ain't no whites,

1283
00:55:17.209 --> 00:55:19.277
there ain't no niggers...
people of color.

1284
00:55:19.311 --> 00:55:21.346
<i>There ain't</i>
<i>none of that.</i>

1285
00:55:21.380 --> 00:55:23.348
At the deep bottom of the mine

1286
00:55:23.382 --> 00:55:25.884
where the gold is at,
there ain't none of that.

1287
00:55:25.918 --> 00:55:28.219
There's just the pure ore,

1288
00:55:28.254 --> 00:55:30.121
that forever thing

1289
00:55:30.156 --> 00:55:31.856
that you don't think is there,

1290
00:55:31.891 --> 00:55:34.526
that thing that keeps people
nailed down to the platform

1291
00:55:34.560 --> 00:55:36.461
when the Sunset Limited
is coming through,

1292
00:55:36.495 --> 00:55:38.430
even if they think
they want to get aboard,

1293
00:55:38.464 --> 00:55:40.832
that thing that allows you

1294
00:55:40.866 --> 00:55:42.867
to ladle out benediction

1295
00:55:42.902 --> 00:55:44.903
on the heads of strangers

1296
00:55:44.937 --> 00:55:47.005
instead of curses.

1297
00:55:47.039 --> 00:55:49.040
And it's all one thing.

1298
00:55:49.075 --> 00:55:52.210
And it ain't but one thing,
just one.

1299
00:55:52.244 --> 00:55:53.778
And that would be Jesus.

1300
00:55:53.813 --> 00:55:56.948
I got to think about
how to answer that.

1301
00:56:02.721 --> 00:56:05.723
Maybe one more heresy
won't hurt you.

1302
00:56:05.758 --> 00:56:08.793
Pretty loaded up
on them already.

1303
00:56:08.828 --> 00:56:11.396
Here's what I would say.

1304
00:56:11.430 --> 00:56:12.597
I would say...

1305
00:56:12.631 --> 00:56:14.499
Mm!

1306
00:56:14.533 --> 00:56:16.668
I would say

1307
00:56:16.702 --> 00:56:19.137
that thing we're
talking about is Jesus,

1308
00:56:19.171 --> 00:56:20.972
but Jesus as understood

1309
00:56:21.006 --> 00:56:23.441
as that gold at the
bottom of the mine.

1310
00:56:23.476 --> 00:56:25.710
<i>He could not</i>
<i>come down here</i>

1311
00:56:25.744 --> 00:56:27.846
and assume the shape of a man

1312
00:56:27.880 --> 00:56:31.583
if that form was not done
shaped to accommodate him.

1313
00:56:31.617 --> 00:56:34.219
And if I said ain't no way
for Jesus to be every man

1314
00:56:34.253 --> 00:56:35.920
without every man being Jesus,

1315
00:56:35.955 --> 00:56:38.857
well now, I think that would
be a pretty big heresy.

1316
00:56:38.891 --> 00:56:40.959
But that's all right.
It ain't as big a heresy

1317
00:56:40.993 --> 00:56:43.962
as saying like man ain't
no different from a rock,

1318
00:56:43.996 --> 00:56:46.397
which is how I see
your point of view.

1319
00:56:46.432 --> 00:56:48.867
It's not my point of view.
I believe in the primacy of the intellect.

1320
00:56:48.901 --> 00:56:50.535
Mm, and what's that word?

1321
00:56:50.569 --> 00:56:53.304
Primacy? It means first.
It means what you put first.

1322
00:56:53.339 --> 00:56:56.241
And that would be intellect?

1323
00:56:56.275 --> 00:56:57.809
Yes.

1324
00:56:57.843 --> 00:56:59.944
Well, what about the primacy
of the Sunset Limited?

1325
00:56:59.979 --> 00:57:01.513
Yes, that too.

1326
00:57:01.547 --> 00:57:04.816
But not the primacy of the
people waiting on a later train?

1327
00:57:04.850 --> 00:57:07.652
No, no primacy there.

1328
00:57:07.686 --> 00:57:11.055
Hmm.

1329
00:57:11.090 --> 00:57:13.491
You tough, Professor.

1330
00:57:13.526 --> 00:57:16.361
You tough.

1331
00:57:19.331 --> 00:57:22.133
You see yourself
as a questioner.

1332
00:57:22.168 --> 00:57:25.203
<i>But about that</i>
<i>I got my doubts.</i>

1333
00:57:25.237 --> 00:57:29.240
The quest of your life
is your quest.

1334
00:57:29.275 --> 00:57:31.409
And you're on a road
that you laid.

1335
00:57:31.443 --> 00:57:34.846
And that fact alone might be all the
reason you need for sticking to it.

1336
00:57:34.880 --> 00:57:38.016
<i>As long as you're on that road</i>
<i>you can't get lost.</i>

1337
00:57:38.050 --> 00:57:40.552
I'm not sure I understand
what you're saying.

1338
00:57:40.586 --> 00:57:43.188
Oh, I got my doubts about
you not understanding

1339
00:57:43.222 --> 00:57:45.557
anything I say, Professor.

1340
00:57:45.591 --> 00:57:47.692
I'm fixing to say grace.

1341
00:57:47.726 --> 00:57:50.862
Lord, we thank you
for this food

1342
00:57:50.896 --> 00:57:54.132
and for the many blessings we
have received from your hand.

1343
00:57:54.166 --> 00:57:56.935
We thank you for the life of the
Professor which you have returned to us

1344
00:57:56.969 --> 00:58:00.405
and ask that you would look
after him, 'cause we need him.

1345
00:58:00.439 --> 00:58:01.873
Now I don't know
why we need him.

1346
00:58:01.907 --> 00:58:04.242
<i>I just know we do.</i>

1347
00:58:04.276 --> 00:58:06.878
Amen.

1348
00:58:06.912 --> 00:58:08.947
Tell me how you like this.

1349
00:58:08.981 --> 00:58:10.882
It looks good.

1350
00:58:14.887 --> 00:58:16.521
This is good.

1351
00:58:19.625 --> 00:58:22.060
This is very good.

1352
00:58:22.094 --> 00:58:24.229
Supposed to be good.
It's soul food, my man.

1353
00:58:24.263 --> 00:58:26.497
It's got what in it?
Molasses?

1354
00:58:26.532 --> 00:58:28.333
- Mm, you a chef, Professor?
- Not really.

1355
00:58:28.367 --> 00:58:29.801
- But some?
- Some, yes.

1356
00:58:29.835 --> 00:58:33.137
Bananas, of course.
Mangos?

1357
00:58:33.172 --> 00:58:36.274
- Got a mango or two in it, rutabagas.
- Rutabagas?

1358
00:58:36.308 --> 00:58:38.509
Rutabagas.
Them's hard to find.

1359
00:58:38.544 --> 00:58:40.511
It's very good.

1360
00:58:40.546 --> 00:58:43.715
You know, it gets better
after a day or two.

1361
00:58:43.749 --> 00:58:45.650
I just fixed this last night.

1362
00:58:45.684 --> 00:58:48.453
You gotta heat it up a few times
to get the flavors right.

1363
00:58:48.487 --> 00:58:49.587
Like chili.

1364
00:58:49.622 --> 00:58:51.389
Like chili.
Yeah, that's right.

1365
00:58:51.423 --> 00:58:53.424
You know where I
learned to fix this?

1366
00:58:53.459 --> 00:58:54.959
- In Louisiana?
- Mm-mm.

1367
00:58:54.994 --> 00:58:57.595
Right here in the ghettos
of New York City.

1368
00:58:57.630 --> 00:59:01.266
There's a lot of different
influences in a dish like this.

1369
00:59:01.300 --> 00:59:03.101
Many parts of the world
in that pot over yonder,

1370
00:59:03.135 --> 00:59:05.203
<i>a lot of different countries,</i>
<i>different people.</i>

1371
00:59:05.237 --> 00:59:06.871
Any white people?

1372
00:59:06.905 --> 00:59:08.406
Not if you can help it.

1373
00:59:08.440 --> 00:59:09.707
Really?

1374
00:59:09.742 --> 00:59:11.576
I'm just messing
with you, Professor,

1375
00:59:11.610 --> 00:59:13.311
just messing with you.

1376
00:59:13.345 --> 00:59:15.346
You know them chefs
in them uptown restaurants?

1377
00:59:15.381 --> 00:59:17.882
- Not personally, no.
- <i>You know what they like to fix?</i>

1378
00:59:17.916 --> 00:59:19.450
- No.
- <i>Sweetbreads...</i>

1379
00:59:19.485 --> 00:59:22.153
Brains, tripe, all that shit don't
nobody like to eat.

1380
00:59:22.187 --> 00:59:23.588
You know why that is?

1381
00:59:23.622 --> 00:59:26.324
Because it's a challenge.
You have to innovate.

1382
00:59:26.358 --> 00:59:28.059
You're pretty smart
for a honky.

1383
00:59:28.093 --> 00:59:29.694
That's right.
It's a challenge.

1384
00:59:29.728 --> 00:59:31.763
<i>The stuff they fix...</i>
<i>dead cheap.</i>

1385
00:59:31.797 --> 00:59:34.198
Most folks throw it out,
give it to the cat.

1386
00:59:34.233 --> 00:59:36.801
But poor folks...
They don't throw nothing out.

1387
00:59:36.835 --> 00:59:38.169
I guess that's right.

1388
00:59:38.203 --> 00:59:40.505
Anybody can make a porterhouse
steak taste good.

1389
00:59:40.539 --> 00:59:42.373
But if you can't buy
no porterhouse steak

1390
00:59:42.408 --> 00:59:45.009
and you still wants to eat something
that tastes good, what do you do then?

1391
00:59:45.044 --> 00:59:46.744
- Innovate.
- <i>Innovate. That's right.</i>

1392
00:59:46.779 --> 00:59:48.479
And who is it
that's got to innovate?

1393
00:59:48.514 --> 00:59:51.182
- Poor people.
- <i>That's right. Poor people.</i>

1394
00:59:51.216 --> 00:59:53.618
You fixing to get an a+.

1395
00:59:53.652 --> 00:59:55.787
So how you like this?

1396
00:59:55.821 --> 00:59:58.289
It's very good.

1397
01:00:02.494 --> 01:00:05.596
You don't think a glass of wine
would have been good with this?

1398
01:00:05.631 --> 01:00:07.432
I do think a glass of wine
would have been good with it.

1399
01:00:07.466 --> 01:00:08.966
<i>But you wouldn't</i>
<i>drink it?</i>

1400
01:00:09.001 --> 01:00:11.769
Oh, I might.
Just one glass.

1401
01:00:11.804 --> 01:00:14.572
Jesus drank wine...
he and his disciples.

1402
01:00:14.606 --> 01:00:17.108
Yes, he did. Says so
right here in the Bible.

1403
01:00:17.142 --> 01:00:19.477
Of course it don't say nothing
about him hiding it in the toilet.

1404
01:00:19.511 --> 01:00:21.446
Is that really a favorite
hiding place?

1405
01:00:21.480 --> 01:00:23.448
Oh yeah.
I've known drunks

1406
01:00:23.482 --> 01:00:25.483
to lift the lids off toilets

1407
01:00:25.517 --> 01:00:27.452
in strange places
just on the off chance.

1408
01:00:27.486 --> 01:00:29.087
- Is that true?
- No.

1409
01:00:29.121 --> 01:00:31.622
It could be though.
Wouldn't surprise me none.

1410
01:00:34.226 --> 01:00:36.394
What is the worst thing
you ever did?

1411
01:00:40.566 --> 01:00:42.867
- More jailhouse stories?
- Why not?

1412
01:00:42.901 --> 01:00:44.669
Which why not you want to hear?

1413
01:00:44.703 --> 01:00:48.172
Is bludgeoning the man in the prison
cafeteria the worst thing you ever did?

1414
01:00:48.207 --> 01:00:49.741
No, it ain't.

1415
01:00:49.775 --> 01:00:52.076
It isn't?
What's the worst?

1416
01:00:52.111 --> 01:00:54.145
- I ain't gonna tell you.
- Why not?

1417
01:00:54.179 --> 01:00:56.981
'Cause you'll jump up, run
out the door hollering.

1418
01:00:57.015 --> 01:00:58.549
It must be pretty bad.

1419
01:00:58.584 --> 01:01:00.585
It is. That's why
I ain't telling you.

1420
01:01:00.619 --> 01:01:03.688
- <i>Now I'm afraid to ask.</i>
- No, u ain't.

1421
01:01:03.722 --> 01:01:05.123
Did you ever tell anyone?

1422
01:01:05.157 --> 01:01:07.558
Yeah.

1423
01:01:07.593 --> 01:01:09.594
<i>It wouldn't</i>
<i>leave me alone.</i>

1424
01:01:09.628 --> 01:01:11.095
Who did you tell it to?

1425
01:01:11.130 --> 01:01:13.197
I told it to a man of God
who was my friend.

1426
01:01:13.232 --> 01:01:15.767
- <i>What did he say?</i>
- He didn't say a word.

1427
01:01:15.801 --> 01:01:18.603
But you're not curious about
the worst thing I ever did.

1428
01:01:18.637 --> 01:01:20.371
Yeah, I am.

1429
01:01:20.406 --> 01:01:21.906
<i>But you won't ask me</i>
<i>what it is.</i>

1430
01:01:21.940 --> 01:01:23.541
- I don't have to.
- Why is that?

1431
01:01:23.575 --> 01:01:26.077
'Cause I was there.
I seen it.

1432
01:01:26.111 --> 01:01:29.080
Well, I might have
a different view.

1433
01:01:29.114 --> 01:01:31.349
Yeah, you might.

1434
01:01:32.785 --> 01:01:34.285
You want some more?

1435
01:01:34.319 --> 01:01:36.621
No, I'm stuffed.

1436
01:01:36.655 --> 01:01:38.022
Hungrier than you thought?

1437
01:01:38.056 --> 01:01:39.390
Yes, I was.

1438
01:01:39.425 --> 01:01:40.758
Good.

1439
01:01:50.502 --> 01:01:52.470
Is this some kind of
test of your faith?

1440
01:01:52.504 --> 01:01:55.006
- What, you?
- Me, yes.

1441
01:01:55.040 --> 01:01:58.209
It ain't <i>my</i> faith
you're testing.

1442
01:01:58.243 --> 01:02:00.945
You see everything
in black and white.

1443
01:02:00.979 --> 01:02:02.680
It is black and white.

1444
01:02:02.714 --> 01:02:05.383
<i>I suppose that makes the world</i>
<i>easier to understand.</i>

1445
01:02:05.417 --> 01:02:07.385
You'd be surprised
how little time I spend

1446
01:02:07.419 --> 01:02:08.719
trying to understand the world.

1447
01:02:08.754 --> 01:02:10.888
You try to understand God.

1448
01:02:10.923 --> 01:02:12.857
No, I don't.

1449
01:02:12.891 --> 01:02:15.159
I try to understand
what he wants from me.

1450
01:02:15.194 --> 01:02:16.861
And that's everything you need?

1451
01:02:16.895 --> 01:02:20.031
If God ain't
everything you need,

1452
01:02:20.065 --> 01:02:22.400
then you in a world of trouble.

1453
01:02:22.434 --> 01:02:24.402
I don't make a move
without Jesus.

1454
01:02:24.436 --> 01:02:26.137
When I get up in the morning,

1455
01:02:26.171 --> 01:02:28.706
I try to grab ahold
to his belt.

1456
01:02:28.740 --> 01:02:32.143
Sometime I go into
a manual override.

1457
01:02:32.177 --> 01:02:33.444
I catch myself.

1458
01:02:33.479 --> 01:02:35.713
- Manual override?
- Yeah, you like that?

1459
01:02:35.747 --> 01:02:38.382
- It's okay.
- I thought it was pretty good.

1460
01:02:38.417 --> 01:02:40.985
So you come to the end
of your rope

1461
01:02:41.019 --> 01:02:43.754
and you admit defeat
and you're in despair.

1462
01:02:43.789 --> 01:02:47.325
And in this state you seize
upon this, whatever it is,

1463
01:02:47.359 --> 01:02:49.260
that has neither sense
nor substance,

1464
01:02:49.294 --> 01:02:53.130
and you grab hold of it
and hang on for dear life.

1465
01:02:53.165 --> 01:02:54.465
Is that a fair portrayal?

1466
01:02:54.500 --> 01:02:56.000
That might be
one way to say it.

1467
01:02:56.034 --> 01:02:58.769
It doesn't make any sense.

1468
01:02:58.804 --> 01:03:00.671
Well, I thought when
we was talking earlier

1469
01:03:00.706 --> 01:03:02.840
you was saying that there was none of it
made no sense...

1470
01:03:02.875 --> 01:03:05.009
Talking about the history
of the world and some such.

1471
01:03:05.043 --> 01:03:07.178
Well, it doesn't
on a larger scale.

1472
01:03:07.212 --> 01:03:09.947
But what you're telling me
is not a view of things.

1473
01:03:09.982 --> 01:03:12.450
It's a view of one thing
and I find it nonsensical.

1474
01:03:12.484 --> 01:03:14.418
What would you do
if Jesus was to speak to you?

1475
01:03:14.453 --> 01:03:16.554
Do you imagine that he might?

1476
01:03:16.588 --> 01:03:18.155
<i>No, I don't.</i>
<i>But I don't know.</i>

1477
01:03:18.190 --> 01:03:20.124
I'm not virtuous enough.

1478
01:03:20.158 --> 01:03:22.360
No, Professor,
it ain't nothing like that.

1479
01:03:22.394 --> 01:03:24.095
You ain't got to be virtuous.

1480
01:03:24.129 --> 01:03:26.063
You just has to be quiet.

1481
01:03:26.098 --> 01:03:28.366
Now I can't speak for the lord,

1482
01:03:28.400 --> 01:03:30.368
but it has been my experience

1483
01:03:30.402 --> 01:03:33.704
that he will talk to
anybody who will listen.

1484
01:03:33.739 --> 01:03:35.573
<i>You damn sure ain't got</i>
<i>to be virtuous.</i>

1485
01:03:35.607 --> 01:03:37.642
If I heard God talking to me,

1486
01:03:37.676 --> 01:03:39.810
I would be ready for you
to take me up to Bellevue,

1487
01:03:39.845 --> 01:03:41.078
as you suggested.

1488
01:03:41.113 --> 01:03:42.713
What if what he said
made sense?

1489
01:03:42.748 --> 01:03:45.716
Wouldn't make any difference.
Craziness is craziness.

1490
01:03:45.751 --> 01:03:48.920
- Don't make no difference if it makes sense?
- No.

1491
01:03:48.954 --> 01:03:52.223
Well, that's about as big a case
of the primacy as I ever heard.

1492
01:03:52.257 --> 01:03:54.392
I've always gone my own way.

1493
01:03:54.426 --> 01:03:56.494
<i>Ich kann nicht anders.</i>

1494
01:03:56.528 --> 01:03:57.662
What's that you talking?

1495
01:03:57.696 --> 01:04:00.097
- It's German.
- You talk German?

1496
01:04:00.132 --> 01:04:01.966
Not really. A little.
It's a quotation.

1497
01:04:02.000 --> 01:04:04.502
Ah well, it didn't do them Germans
much good though, did it?

1498
01:04:04.536 --> 01:04:06.470
I don't know.
The Germans contributed

1499
01:04:06.505 --> 01:04:09.473
a great deal to
civilization before Hitler.

1500
01:04:09.508 --> 01:04:11.642
And then they
contributed Hitler.

1501
01:04:11.677 --> 01:04:13.611
Yeah, if you like.

1502
01:04:13.645 --> 01:04:17.181
I gather it to be your belief

1503
01:04:17.215 --> 01:04:20.451
that culture tends to
contribute to human misery,

1504
01:04:20.485 --> 01:04:24.488
that the more one knows, the
unhappier one is likely to be.

1505
01:04:24.523 --> 01:04:27.158
As in the case of certain
parties known to us.

1506
01:04:27.192 --> 01:04:28.893
As in the case.

1507
01:04:28.927 --> 01:04:30.428
Well, I don't believe
I said that.

1508
01:04:30.462 --> 01:04:32.296
I think maybe it was you
who said it.

1509
01:04:32.331 --> 01:04:33.931
- I never said it.
- Do you believe it?

1510
01:04:33.966 --> 01:04:35.299
- No.
- No?

1511
01:04:35.334 --> 01:04:37.001
I don't know.
It could be true.

1512
01:04:37.035 --> 01:04:39.570
Well, why is that?
That don't seem right, do it?

1513
01:04:39.605 --> 01:04:42.006
It's the first thing
in that book there...

1514
01:04:42.040 --> 01:04:43.874
<i>the Garden of Eden,</i>

1515
01:04:43.909 --> 01:04:46.777
knowledge as destructive to the
spirit, destructive to goodness.

1516
01:04:46.812 --> 01:04:48.646
I thought I ain't read
that book.

1517
01:04:48.680 --> 01:04:51.048
Everyone knows that story.
It's probably the most famous story in there.

1518
01:04:51.083 --> 01:04:52.650
Hmm, why you think that is?

1519
01:04:52.684 --> 01:04:55.786
I suppose from the God's point
of view all knowledge is vanity.

1520
01:04:55.821 --> 01:04:58.422
Or maybe it just gives people

1521
01:04:58.457 --> 01:05:01.425
the unhealthy illusion that
they can outwit the devil.

1522
01:05:01.460 --> 01:05:03.127
Damn, Professor.

1523
01:05:03.161 --> 01:05:05.162
Where was you
when I needed you?

1524
01:05:05.197 --> 01:05:07.431
You'd better be careful.
You see where it's gotten me.

1525
01:05:07.466 --> 01:05:10.735
Oh, I see.
That's the topic of discussion here.

1526
01:05:13.472 --> 01:05:16.707
The darker picture
is always the correct one.

1527
01:05:16.742 --> 01:05:18.576
When you read the history
of the world

1528
01:05:18.610 --> 01:05:22.113
you are reading a saga of
bloodshed and greed and folly

1529
01:05:22.147 --> 01:05:24.949
the import of which
is impossible to ignore.

1530
01:05:24.983 --> 01:05:27.985
And yet we imagine

1531
01:05:28.020 --> 01:05:30.755
that the future will
somehow be different.

1532
01:05:30.789 --> 01:05:32.957
I've no idea why we are
even still here.

1533
01:05:32.991 --> 01:05:35.326
In all probability we won't
be here much longer.

1534
01:05:35.360 --> 01:05:38.129
Them's some pretty
powerful words, Professor.

1535
01:05:38.163 --> 01:05:40.064
That's what's in your
heart though, ain't it?

1536
01:05:40.098 --> 01:05:42.667
Yes.

1537
01:05:42.701 --> 01:05:44.935
All right, well, I can
relate to them thoughts.

1538
01:05:44.970 --> 01:05:46.837
- You can?
- Sure I can.

1539
01:05:46.872 --> 01:05:48.272
That surprises me.

1540
01:05:48.306 --> 01:05:51.275
But you could be wrong,
you know.

1541
01:05:51.309 --> 01:05:52.743
I don't think so.

1542
01:05:52.778 --> 01:05:55.012
That ain't something you have
a lot of in your life, is it?

1543
01:05:55.047 --> 01:05:56.547
- What isn't?
- Being wrong.

1544
01:05:56.581 --> 01:06:00.818
- I admit it when I'm wrong.
- Oh, I don't know about that.

1545
01:06:00.852 --> 01:06:03.354
Well, you're entitled
to your own opinion.

1546
01:06:08.560 --> 01:06:10.661
<i>Oh yeah,</i>
<i>here it is.</i>

1547
01:06:10.696 --> 01:06:12.263
<i>Story on page three.</i>

1548
01:06:12.297 --> 01:06:14.965
Ah.

1549
01:06:15.000 --> 01:06:16.934
"Friends reported

1550
01:06:16.968 --> 01:06:20.137
that the man had
ignored all advice

1551
01:06:20.172 --> 01:06:22.473
and stated that he intended

1552
01:06:22.507 --> 01:06:24.809
to pursue his own course.

1553
01:06:24.843 --> 01:06:27.478
<i>A close confidant</i>
<i>stated... "</i>

1554
01:06:27.512 --> 01:06:29.847
and this here
is in quotations...

1555
01:06:29.881 --> 01:06:34.618
"You couldn't tell that
son of a bitch nothing."

1556
01:06:34.653 --> 01:06:37.088
Now can you say that
in the paper...

1557
01:06:37.122 --> 01:06:39.190
"Son of a bitch"?

1558
01:06:44.196 --> 01:06:47.732
"Blood-spattered spectators
at the 155th Street station...

1559
01:06:47.766 --> 01:06:51.435
continued on page four..."

1560
01:06:51.470 --> 01:06:54.472
155th Street station...

1561
01:06:54.506 --> 01:06:58.375
"who were interviewed
at the scene said

1562
01:06:58.410 --> 01:07:01.212
that the man's last words

1563
01:07:01.246 --> 01:07:05.950
as he hurtled toward the
oncoming commuter train were,

1564
01:07:05.984 --> 01:07:10.187
'I am right.'"

1565
01:07:14.626 --> 01:07:16.827
very funny.

1566
01:07:18.764 --> 01:07:22.700
Oh, Professor,
you an amazing man.

1567
01:07:22.734 --> 01:07:24.668
I'm glad you find me
entertaining.

1568
01:07:24.703 --> 01:07:27.104
- Oh, I think you're pretty special.
- <i>I don't think I'm special.</i>

1569
01:07:27.139 --> 01:07:29.240
- <i>You don't?</i>
- No, I don't.

1570
01:07:29.274 --> 01:07:31.542
You don't think you view
those other commuters

1571
01:07:31.576 --> 01:07:33.077
from a certain height?

1572
01:07:33.111 --> 01:07:36.013
I view those other commuters
as fellow occupants

1573
01:07:36.047 --> 01:07:38.249
of the same abyssal pit
in which I find myself.

1574
01:07:38.283 --> 01:07:40.184
And if they see it
as something different,

1575
01:07:40.218 --> 01:07:42.453
I don't know how that
makes me special.

1576
01:07:42.487 --> 01:07:43.721
Mm.

1577
01:07:48.560 --> 01:07:50.494
I hear what you're saying.

1578
01:07:50.529 --> 01:07:53.497
But still I keep coming
back to these commuters,

1579
01:07:53.532 --> 01:07:56.167
them that's waiting
on the Sunset.

1580
01:07:56.201 --> 01:07:58.402
I can't help but think
there's got to be something

1581
01:07:58.436 --> 01:08:00.938
a little special
about theirselves.

1582
01:08:00.972 --> 01:08:04.275
I mean, they got to be
in a deeper pit

1583
01:08:04.309 --> 01:08:07.311
than us day travelers...
deeper and darker.

1584
01:08:07.345 --> 01:08:09.079
I ain't saying they
down as deep as you,

1585
01:08:09.114 --> 01:08:11.248
- but pretty deep maybe.
- So?

1586
01:08:11.283 --> 01:08:13.651
So maybe they your brothers

1587
01:08:13.685 --> 01:08:17.521
in self-destruction
and despair.

1588
01:08:17.556 --> 01:08:19.523
I thought misery loved company.

1589
01:08:19.558 --> 01:08:21.826
I'm sure I don't know.

1590
01:08:21.860 --> 01:08:25.329
Let me take a shot at it.

1591
01:08:25.363 --> 01:08:27.331
What I think is

1592
01:08:27.365 --> 01:08:29.867
that you got better
reasons than them.

1593
01:08:29.901 --> 01:08:32.903
See, their reasons is that
they just don't like it here,

1594
01:08:32.938 --> 01:08:35.706
but yours says
what it is not to like

1595
01:08:35.740 --> 01:08:38.075
and why not not to like it.

1596
01:08:38.109 --> 01:08:41.846
You got more intelligent
reasons, more elegant reasons.

1597
01:08:41.880 --> 01:08:43.714
- You making fun of me?
- No, I ain't.

1598
01:08:43.748 --> 01:08:46.183
- But you think I'm full of shit?
- No, I don't think that.

1599
01:08:46.218 --> 01:08:49.486
But I don't doubt that it's possible
to die from being full of shit.

1600
01:08:49.521 --> 01:08:51.455
But I don't think that's
what we're looking at here.

1601
01:08:51.489 --> 01:08:53.090
What do you think
we're looking at?

1602
01:08:53.124 --> 01:08:57.261
I don't know.
You got me in uncharted territory.

1603
01:08:57.295 --> 01:09:00.164
You got these
world-class reasons

1604
01:09:00.198 --> 01:09:02.867
for taking the Limited

1605
01:09:02.901 --> 01:09:04.835
where these other dudes...
all they got is,

1606
01:09:04.870 --> 01:09:06.871
well, maybe they just
don't feel good.

1607
01:09:06.905 --> 01:09:09.740
It might could be that you
ain't even all that unhappy.

1608
01:09:09.774 --> 01:09:12.109
You think my education
is driving me to suicide?

1609
01:09:12.143 --> 01:09:14.445
No, I'm just posing
the question.

1610
01:09:14.479 --> 01:09:16.747
Well, wait a minute
'fore you answer.

1611
01:09:22.020 --> 01:09:23.921
Okay, go ahead.

1612
01:09:23.955 --> 01:09:26.590
I think that's the most
ridiculous thing I ever heard.

1613
01:09:30.028 --> 01:09:32.529
"I think that's the most
ridiculous thing I ever heard."

1614
01:09:32.564 --> 01:09:34.598
Very clever.
What's the point?

1615
01:09:34.633 --> 01:09:37.868
The light is all around you

1616
01:09:37.903 --> 01:09:39.770
but you don't see
nothing but shadow.

1617
01:09:39.804 --> 01:09:41.438
And you're the one causing it.

1618
01:09:41.473 --> 01:09:43.807
It's you.
You're the shadow.

1619
01:09:43.842 --> 01:09:45.142
That's the point.

1620
01:09:45.176 --> 01:09:46.744
Well, I don't have your faith.

1621
01:09:46.778 --> 01:09:48.512
Why don't we just
leave it at that?

1622
01:09:48.546 --> 01:09:50.614
You ain't never thought
about just starting over?

1623
01:09:50.649 --> 01:09:52.616
I did at one time.
I don't anymore.

1624
01:09:52.651 --> 01:09:55.119
Well, maybe faith
is just a case

1625
01:09:55.153 --> 01:09:57.955
of not having
nothing else left.

1626
01:09:57.989 --> 01:10:02.693
Well, I do have something else.

1627
01:10:02.727 --> 01:10:05.663
Well, why don't you just
keep that in reserve?

1628
01:10:05.697 --> 01:10:09.333
<i>Just take a shot</i>
<i>at starting over.</i>

1629
01:10:09.367 --> 01:10:11.936
I don't mean starting again.
Everybody's done that.

1630
01:10:11.970 --> 01:10:15.272
Over means over.
Just walk away.

1631
01:10:15.307 --> 01:10:19.310
I mean, if everything you are

1632
01:10:19.344 --> 01:10:22.279
and everything you have
and everything you done

1633
01:10:22.314 --> 01:10:25.182
has brought you at last to the
bottom of a whisky bottle

1634
01:10:25.216 --> 01:10:28.252
or bought you a one-way
ticket on the Sunset Limited,

1635
01:10:28.286 --> 01:10:32.289
you can't give me one good
reason on God's green earth

1636
01:10:32.324 --> 01:10:36.260
for salvaging none of it,
'cause there ain't none.

1637
01:10:36.294 --> 01:10:38.262
If you can bring yourself

1638
01:10:38.296 --> 01:10:41.899
to close the door
on all of that,

1639
01:10:41.933 --> 01:10:45.402
it will be cold
and it will be lonely

1640
01:10:45.437 --> 01:10:49.406
and there will be
a fierce wind blowing.

1641
01:10:49.441 --> 01:10:52.543
But you don't say nothing.

1642
01:10:52.577 --> 01:10:55.913
You just turn your collar up

1643
01:10:55.947 --> 01:10:57.548
and keep walking.

1644
01:10:57.582 --> 01:11:01.218
I can't.
I can't.

1645
01:11:05.223 --> 01:11:06.724
You want some more coffee?

1646
01:11:06.758 --> 01:11:08.892
No, thank you.

1647
01:11:12.430 --> 01:11:14.932
Why do you think it is
folks take their own life?

1648
01:11:14.966 --> 01:11:17.034
I don't know.
Different reasons.

1649
01:11:17.068 --> 01:11:19.470
Is there anything these different
reasons has got in common?

1650
01:11:19.504 --> 01:11:21.472
I can't speak for others.

1651
01:11:21.506 --> 01:11:23.440
My own reasons center around

1652
01:11:23.475 --> 01:11:25.442
a gradual loss
of make-believe,

1653
01:11:25.477 --> 01:11:28.045
a gradual enlightenment
as to the nature

1654
01:11:28.079 --> 01:11:31.048
of reality, of the world.

1655
01:11:31.082 --> 01:11:33.517
- Them worldly reasons.
- <i>If you like.</i>

1656
01:11:33.551 --> 01:11:36.520
- Them elegant reasons.
- That was your description.

1657
01:11:36.554 --> 01:11:38.355
<i>You ain't disagreed</i>
<i>with it.</i>

1658
01:11:38.390 --> 01:11:41.892
It's them reasons that your
brother don't know nothing about

1659
01:11:41.926 --> 01:11:45.662
hanging from his necktie from a
steampipe down in the basement.

1660
01:11:45.697 --> 01:11:48.032
He's got his own
dumbass reasons.

1661
01:11:48.066 --> 01:11:51.168
But if we could find a way
to educate him

1662
01:11:51.202 --> 01:11:53.170
to them more elegant reasons

1663
01:11:53.204 --> 01:11:55.973
and make them available
to him and his buddies,

1664
01:11:56.007 --> 01:11:57.941
then there'd be a lot
of folk out there

1665
01:11:57.976 --> 01:12:01.145
could off themselves with more joy
in their hearts. What you think?

1666
01:12:01.179 --> 01:12:03.447
Now I know
you're being facetious.

1667
01:12:03.481 --> 01:12:06.917
I think you're right.
I think you done finally drove me to it.

1668
01:12:06.951 --> 01:12:08.619
Mm-hmm.

1669
01:12:08.653 --> 01:12:12.356
Professor done gone
to laying mm-hmms on me.

1670
01:12:12.390 --> 01:12:13.724
I guess I'd better
watch my step.

1671
01:12:13.758 --> 01:12:15.526
You better had.
I might be warming up the trick bag.

1672
01:12:15.560 --> 01:12:19.096
You think your reasons
is about the world,

1673
01:12:19.130 --> 01:12:21.165
and his is mostly about him.

1674
01:12:21.199 --> 01:12:23.467
I think that's probably true.

1675
01:12:23.501 --> 01:12:26.904
I think I see a different truth
sitting across the table from me.

1676
01:12:26.938 --> 01:12:28.439
<i>Which is?</i>

1677
01:12:28.473 --> 01:12:31.141
That you must
love your brother or die.

1678
01:12:31.176 --> 01:12:32.843
I don't know what that means.

1679
01:12:32.877 --> 01:12:35.679
It's another world
from anything I know.

1680
01:12:35.713 --> 01:12:38.449
Well, well, tell me
how your world is.

1681
01:12:38.516 --> 01:12:39.817
You don't want to hr it.

1682
01:12:39.851 --> 01:12:41.819
- Sure I do.
- I don't think so.

1683
01:12:41.853 --> 01:12:44.955
Yeah, go ahead.

1684
01:12:44.989 --> 01:12:46.924
All right.

1685
01:12:46.958 --> 01:12:49.827
It's that the world is
basically a forced labor camp

1686
01:12:49.861 --> 01:12:52.062
<i>from which the workers,</i>
<i>perfectly innocent,</i>

1687
01:12:52.097 --> 01:12:54.431
are led forth by lottery,
a few each day,

1688
01:12:54.466 --> 01:12:56.400
to be executed.

1689
01:12:56.434 --> 01:12:58.769
I don't think that's
just the way I see it.

1690
01:12:58.803 --> 01:13:00.771
I think that's the way it is.

1691
01:13:00.805 --> 01:13:03.373
Are there alternative
views? Yes.

1692
01:13:03.408 --> 01:13:05.909
Will any of them
stand close scrutiny?

1693
01:13:05.944 --> 01:13:07.511
No.

1694
01:13:08.480 --> 01:13:11.115
So do you want to take a look

1695
01:13:11.149 --> 01:13:13.851
at that train schedule again?

1696
01:13:21.459 --> 01:13:24.394
If this ain't the life
you wanted, what was?

1697
01:13:24.429 --> 01:13:26.864
I don't know.
Not this.

1698
01:13:26.898 --> 01:13:29.933
Are you living the life
you had planned?

1699
01:13:29.968 --> 01:13:31.368
No, it ain't.

1700
01:13:31.402 --> 01:13:33.770
But I got what I needed
instead of what I wanted.

1701
01:13:33.805 --> 01:13:35.806
Sometimes that's the best
kind of luck to have.

1702
01:13:35.840 --> 01:13:37.741
Yeah, well...

1703
01:13:37.775 --> 01:13:42.379
You can't compare your life
to mine though, can you?

1704
01:13:42.413 --> 01:13:45.983
<i>No, I can't.</i>
<i>( Chuckles</i>

1705
01:13:46.017 --> 01:13:47.751
<i>I just can't.</i>

1706
01:13:47.785 --> 01:13:51.722
Oh, I'm sorry.
I should go.

1707
01:13:51.756 --> 01:13:54.491
- <i>You don't have to go.</i>
- I've offended you.

1708
01:13:54.526 --> 01:13:56.126
Look, my hide is
thicker than that.

1709
01:13:56.161 --> 01:13:58.028
Don't go.
You ain't hurt my feelings.

1710
01:13:58.062 --> 01:14:00.264
I know you think I should be thankful.
I'm sorry not to be.

1711
01:14:00.298 --> 01:14:02.332
- <i>I don't think no such thing.</i>
- I should go.

1712
01:14:02.367 --> 01:14:03.734
I'm digging a dry hole
here, ain't I?

1713
01:14:03.768 --> 01:14:05.269
I admire your persistence.

1714
01:14:05.303 --> 01:14:07.938
What can I do to get you
to just stay just a bit?

1715
01:14:07.972 --> 01:14:11.008
Why? Are you hoping that
if I stay long enough, God might talk to me?

1716
01:14:11.042 --> 01:14:13.210
No, I'm hoping
he'll speak to me.

1717
01:14:13.244 --> 01:14:16.847
I know you think I at least owe
you a little more of my time.

1718
01:14:16.881 --> 01:14:18.649
I know I'm ungrateful.

1719
01:14:18.683 --> 01:14:21.218
But ingratitude is not the sin

1720
01:14:21.252 --> 01:14:24.788
to a spiritual bankrupt
that it is to a man of God.

1721
01:14:24.822 --> 01:14:26.456
I don't think you owe me
nothing, Professor.

1722
01:14:26.491 --> 01:14:27.991
- You really think that?
- Yeah, I do.

1723
01:14:28.026 --> 01:14:30.427
Well, you're very kind.
And I wish there was something I could do

1724
01:14:30.461 --> 01:14:32.129
to repay you, but there isn't.

1725
01:14:32.163 --> 01:14:34.665
So why don't we just say goodbye?
You can get on with your life.

1726
01:14:34.699 --> 01:14:37.134
Suppose...

1727
01:14:37.168 --> 01:14:39.236
Suppose I have to tell you
you could wake up tomorrow

1728
01:14:39.270 --> 01:14:41.305
and you wouldn't want to be
jumping in front of no train.

1729
01:14:41.339 --> 01:14:43.173
Suppose all you had to do was ask.
Would you do it?

1730
01:14:43.208 --> 01:14:45.709
That just depends on what
I would have to give up.

1731
01:14:45.743 --> 01:14:47.811
See, I started to write that
down on a piece of paper.

1732
01:14:47.845 --> 01:14:49.279
What is it you think
I'm holding on to?

1733
01:14:49.314 --> 01:14:50.647
I don't know!

1734
01:14:50.682 --> 01:14:53.417
What is it the terminal
commuter cherishes

1735
01:14:53.451 --> 01:14:55.552
- that he would die for?
- I don't know.

1736
01:14:55.587 --> 01:14:59.089
No no.

1737
01:14:59.123 --> 01:15:00.924
You don't.

1738
01:15:00.959 --> 01:15:02.859
You don't want to talk
to me no more, do you?

1739
01:15:02.894 --> 01:15:04.895
I thought you had a thick skin.

1740
01:15:04.929 --> 01:15:07.831
It is, but it ain't
hide to the bone.

1741
01:15:07.865 --> 01:15:11.969
Why do you think there is
something that I won't give up?

1742
01:15:14.606 --> 01:15:16.707
I don't know.

1743
01:15:20.878 --> 01:15:22.813
I think

1744
01:15:22.847 --> 01:15:26.116
any man anxious to get
run over by a train

1745
01:15:26.150 --> 01:15:28.218
has got to have
something on his mind.

1746
01:15:28.253 --> 01:15:30.087
I mean most of us
would just settle

1747
01:15:30.121 --> 01:15:32.222
for maybe a slap
upside the head.

1748
01:15:32.257 --> 01:15:34.591
You say you don't
care about nothing,

1749
01:15:34.626 --> 01:15:36.493
but I don't believe that.

1750
01:15:36.527 --> 01:15:39.263
I don't think death
is ever about nothing.

1751
01:15:39.297 --> 01:15:42.733
You asked me what I think
you're holding on to

1752
01:15:42.767 --> 01:15:45.369
and I got to say I don't know.

1753
01:15:45.403 --> 01:15:47.904
Or maybe I just don't
have the words to say it.

1754
01:15:47.939 --> 01:15:49.606
And maybe <i>you</i> know

1755
01:15:49.641 --> 01:15:51.842
but you just ain't telling.

1756
01:15:51.876 --> 01:15:53.810
What I believe is

1757
01:15:53.845 --> 01:15:55.979
that when you took
your celebrated leap,

1758
01:15:56.014 --> 01:15:57.714
you was taking it with you

1759
01:15:57.749 --> 01:15:59.716
and you was holding on to it,

1760
01:15:59.751 --> 01:16:03.120
holding on for grim death.

1761
01:16:04.455 --> 01:16:07.224
I'm just looking for
the words, Professor.

1762
01:16:07.258 --> 01:16:09.860
I'm looking for the words
'cause I know the words

1763
01:16:09.894 --> 01:16:12.562
is just the way to your heart.

1764
01:16:12.597 --> 01:16:14.731
Well, I can't help you.

1765
01:16:14.766 --> 01:16:16.366
Letting it all go

1766
01:16:16.401 --> 01:16:18.135
is the place I finally got to.

1767
01:16:18.169 --> 01:16:20.470
It took a lot of work to get there.
And if there's one thing

1768
01:16:20.505 --> 01:16:22.906
I would be unwilling to give
up, it is exactly that.

1769
01:16:22.940 --> 01:16:25.108
Is there another way
you could say that?

1770
01:16:25.143 --> 01:16:28.178
The one thing I won't give up
is giving up.

1771
01:16:28.212 --> 01:16:30.147
I expect that
to carry me through.

1772
01:16:30.181 --> 01:16:32.316
I'm depending on it.

1773
01:16:32.350 --> 01:16:34.318
The things I believe in

1774
01:16:34.352 --> 01:16:36.353
were very frail, as I've said.

1775
01:16:36.387 --> 01:16:38.555
They won't be around very
long and neither will I.

1776
01:16:38.589 --> 01:16:41.658
But I don't think that's really
the reason for my decision.

1777
01:16:41.693 --> 01:16:44.161
I think it goes
deeper than that.

1778
01:16:44.195 --> 01:16:46.563
You can acclimate
yourself to loss.

1779
01:16:46.597 --> 01:16:49.533
You have to.
I mean...

1780
01:16:49.567 --> 01:16:51.134
You like music, right?

1781
01:16:51.169 --> 01:16:53.170
Yeah yeah, I do.

1782
01:16:53.204 --> 01:16:56.306
Who is the greatest composer
that you know of?

1783
01:16:56.341 --> 01:16:58.308
John Coltrane, hands down.

1784
01:16:58.343 --> 01:17:00.043
Do you think his work
will last forever?

1785
01:17:00.078 --> 01:17:02.145
Well,

1786
01:17:02.180 --> 01:17:04.348
forever's a long time,
Professor.

1787
01:17:04.382 --> 01:17:06.183
So I got to say no.

1788
01:17:06.217 --> 01:17:08.418
That doesn't mean
it's worthless, does it?

1789
01:17:08.453 --> 01:17:10.587
No, it don't.

1790
01:17:10.621 --> 01:17:13.924
You give up the world
line by line.

1791
01:17:13.958 --> 01:17:17.027
You become an accomplice
to your own annihilation.

1792
01:17:17.061 --> 01:17:18.562
There's nothing
you can do about it.

1793
01:17:18.596 --> 01:17:22.399
Everything you do closes a
door somewhere ahead of you.

1794
01:17:22.433 --> 01:17:25.836
Finally there's
only one door left.

1795
01:17:25.870 --> 01:17:28.071
That's a dark world, Professor.

1796
01:17:28.106 --> 01:17:32.242
Maybe you just need to admit
that you're in over your head.

1797
01:17:32.276 --> 01:17:34.911
I do admit it, but that
don't let me off the hook.

1798
01:17:34.946 --> 01:17:38.014
I got no choice.

1799
01:17:38.049 --> 01:17:39.549
Okay.

1800
01:17:39.584 --> 01:17:42.519
Maybe you're right.

1801
01:17:42.553 --> 01:17:47.023
Well, here is my news,
reverend.

1802
01:17:47.058 --> 01:17:49.359
I long for the darkness.

1803
01:17:49.394 --> 01:17:51.795
I pray for death, real death.

1804
01:17:51.829 --> 01:17:54.564
And if I thought that in death I would
meet the people I knew in life,

1805
01:17:54.599 --> 01:17:57.200
I don't know what I would do.
That would be the ultimate horror,

1806
01:17:57.235 --> 01:17:59.169
the ultimate nightmare.
If I thought I was gonna

1807
01:17:59.203 --> 01:18:01.438
meet my mother again and
start all of that all over,

1808
01:18:01.472 --> 01:18:05.308
only this time without the prospect
of death to look forward to,

1809
01:18:05.343 --> 01:18:08.011
that would be
the final nightmare,

1810
01:18:08.045 --> 01:18:10.046
Kafka on wheels.

1811
01:18:10.081 --> 01:18:12.549
Damn, Professor.
You don't want to see your own mama?

1812
01:18:12.583 --> 01:18:14.284
No, I don't.

1813
01:18:14.318 --> 01:18:17.721
I want the dead to be dead

1814
01:18:17.755 --> 01:18:19.689
forever.

1815
01:18:19.724 --> 01:18:21.725
<i>And I want to be</i>
<i>one of them.</i>

1816
01:18:21.759 --> 01:18:24.761
Except of course you can't be one of them.
You can't be one of the dead

1817
01:18:24.796 --> 01:18:27.531
because that which has no
existence can have no community.

1818
01:18:27.565 --> 01:18:30.333
No community.

1819
01:18:30.368 --> 01:18:32.402
My heart warms
just thinking about it...

1820
01:18:32.437 --> 01:18:34.938
blackness, aloneness,

1821
01:18:34.972 --> 01:18:38.775
silence, peace,

1822
01:18:38.810 --> 01:18:41.645
and all of it
only a heartbeat away.

1823
01:18:44.916 --> 01:18:48.585
I don't regard my state of mind

1824
01:18:48.619 --> 01:18:51.221
as some pessimistic
view of the world.

1825
01:18:51.255 --> 01:18:53.857
I regard it as the world itself.

1826
01:18:53.891 --> 01:18:56.760
Evolution cannot avoid

1827
01:18:56.794 --> 01:18:58.795
bringing intelligent life

1828
01:18:58.830 --> 01:19:00.931
ultimately to an awareness
of one thing,

1829
01:19:00.965 --> 01:19:02.399
and one thing above all else.

1830
01:19:02.433 --> 01:19:06.102
And that one thing is futility.

1831
01:19:06.137 --> 01:19:09.372
If I'm understanding you
right, you're saying

1832
01:19:09.407 --> 01:19:12.642
<i>everybody that just ain't</i>
<i>eat up with the dumbass</i>

1833
01:19:12.677 --> 01:19:14.277
<i>ought to be suicidal.</i>

1834
01:19:14.312 --> 01:19:16.446
- Yes.
- You ain't shitting me?

1835
01:19:16.481 --> 01:19:19.716
No, I am not shitting you.

1836
01:19:19.750 --> 01:19:23.019
If people could see the
world for what it truly is,

1837
01:19:23.054 --> 01:19:25.622
see their lives
for what they truly are,

1838
01:19:25.656 --> 01:19:28.358
without dreams or illusions,

1839
01:19:28.392 --> 01:19:30.627
I don't believe they could
offer the first reason

1840
01:19:30.661 --> 01:19:33.597
why they should not elect
to die as soon as possible.

1841
01:19:33.631 --> 01:19:36.399
I don't believe in God.
Can you understand that?

1842
01:19:36.434 --> 01:19:38.535
Look around you, man.
Can't you see?

1843
01:19:38.569 --> 01:19:41.471
<i>The clamor and din</i>
<i>of those in torment</i>

1844
01:19:41.506 --> 01:19:44.241
has to be the sound
most pleasing to his ear.

1845
01:19:44.275 --> 01:19:46.443
And I loathe
these discussions...

1846
01:19:46.477 --> 01:19:48.945
The argument
of the village atheist

1847
01:19:48.980 --> 01:19:52.415
whose single passion
is to revile endlessly

1848
01:19:52.450 --> 01:19:56.052
that which he denies the
existence of in the first place.

1849
01:19:56.087 --> 01:19:57.854
Your fellowship

1850
01:19:57.889 --> 01:20:00.156
<i>is a fellowship of pain</i>
<i>and nothing more.</i>

1851
01:20:00.191 --> 01:20:02.025
<i>And if that pain</i>
<i>were collective</i>

1852
01:20:02.059 --> 01:20:03.727
instead of merely reiterative,

1853
01:20:03.761 --> 01:20:05.962
the sheer weight of it
would drag the world

1854
01:20:05.997 --> 01:20:07.864
from the walls of the universe

1855
01:20:07.899 --> 01:20:10.534
and send it crashing
and burning down

1856
01:20:10.568 --> 01:20:14.104
through whatever night it might
yet be capable of engendering

1857
01:20:14.138 --> 01:20:16.973
until it was not even ash.

1858
01:20:17.008 --> 01:20:20.010
And brotherhood,

1859
01:20:20.044 --> 01:20:24.080
justice, eternal life?

1860
01:20:24.115 --> 01:20:26.283
Good God, man.

1861
01:20:26.317 --> 01:20:28.885
Show me a religion
that prepares one

1862
01:20:28.920 --> 01:20:30.787
for nothingness, for death.

1863
01:20:30.821 --> 01:20:32.455
That's a church I might enter.

1864
01:20:32.490 --> 01:20:36.426
Yours prepares one
only for more life,

1865
01:20:36.460 --> 01:20:40.063
for dreams and illusions
and lies.

1866
01:20:40.097 --> 01:20:42.632
Banish the fear of death
from men's hearts...

1867
01:20:42.667 --> 01:20:46.202
They wouldn't live a day.

1868
01:20:46.237 --> 01:20:48.638
Who would want this nightmare

1869
01:20:48.673 --> 01:20:51.942
but for fear of the next?

1870
01:20:51.976 --> 01:20:54.511
The shadow of the axe

1871
01:20:54.545 --> 01:20:57.647
hangs over every joy.

1872
01:20:57.682 --> 01:21:00.684
Every road ends in death,

1873
01:21:00.718 --> 01:21:03.587
every friendship, every love.

1874
01:21:03.621 --> 01:21:08.224
<i>Torment, loss,</i>
<i>betrayal,</i>

1875
01:21:08.259 --> 01:21:12.462
pain, suffering,

1876
01:21:12.496 --> 01:21:15.131
age,

1877
01:21:15.166 --> 01:21:17.567
indignity,

1878
01:21:17.602 --> 01:21:22.772
hideous lingering illness...

1879
01:21:22.807 --> 01:21:27.510
and all of it
with a single conclusion

1880
01:21:27.545 --> 01:21:29.479
for you

1881
01:21:29.513 --> 01:21:31.548
and every one and every thing

1882
01:21:31.582 --> 01:21:34.851
you have ever chosen
to care for.

1883
01:21:34.885 --> 01:21:37.153
That is the true brotherhood,
the true fellowship.

1884
01:21:37.188 --> 01:21:39.289
And everybody is
a member for life.

1885
01:21:39.323 --> 01:21:43.560
You tell me that my
brother is my salvation?

1886
01:21:43.594 --> 01:21:45.395
My salvation?

1887
01:21:45.429 --> 01:21:47.497
Well, then damn him.

1888
01:21:47.531 --> 01:21:50.967
Damn him in every shape
and guise and form.

1889
01:21:51.002 --> 01:21:53.069
Do I see myself in him?
Yes, I do.

1890
01:21:53.104 --> 01:21:56.673
And what I see sickens me.

1891
01:21:56.707 --> 01:21:59.943
Do you understand me?

1892
01:21:59.977 --> 01:22:02.278
Can you understand me?

1893
01:22:11.255 --> 01:22:13.056
I'm sorry.

1894
01:22:14.992 --> 01:22:16.626
How long you felt like this?

1895
01:22:16.661 --> 01:22:18.762
All my life.

1896
01:22:18.796 --> 01:22:20.897
- Is that true?
- It's worse than that.

1897
01:22:20.931 --> 01:22:23.099
I don't see what could be
worse than that.

1898
01:22:23.134 --> 01:22:27.070
Rage is really only
for the good days.

1899
01:22:27.104 --> 01:22:29.139
The truth is there's
little of that left.

1900
01:22:29.173 --> 01:22:32.709
The truth is
that the forms I see

1901
01:22:32.743 --> 01:22:35.311
have been slowly emptied out.

1902
01:22:35.346 --> 01:22:37.280
They no longer have
any content.

1903
01:22:37.314 --> 01:22:40.383
They're shapes only...
a train, a wall,

1904
01:22:40.418 --> 01:22:43.119
a world, a man...

1905
01:22:43.154 --> 01:22:45.689
a thing dangling

1906
01:22:45.723 --> 01:22:48.692
in senseless articulation
in a howling void,

1907
01:22:48.726 --> 01:22:50.927
no meaning to its life,

1908
01:22:50.961 --> 01:22:53.630
its words.

1909
01:22:56.333 --> 01:23:00.303
Why would I seek out
the company of such a thing?

1910
01:23:00.337 --> 01:23:01.671
Why?

1911
01:23:03.240 --> 01:23:05.575
Damn.

1912
01:23:05.609 --> 01:23:08.478
So you see what it is
you've saved?

1913
01:23:08.512 --> 01:23:10.947
Tried to save.
Still trying, trying hard.

1914
01:23:10.981 --> 01:23:14.084
- Who is your brother?
- Who is my brother, yes.

1915
01:23:14.118 --> 01:23:16.052
Is that the reason I'm here
in your apartment?

1916
01:23:16.087 --> 01:23:18.955
No, that's why I'm here.

1917
01:23:18.989 --> 01:23:21.791
You asked me
what I'm a Professor of.

1918
01:23:21.826 --> 01:23:24.160
I am a Professor of darkness,

1919
01:23:24.195 --> 01:23:27.063
the night in day's clothing.

1920
01:23:27.098 --> 01:23:30.133
And now I wish you all
the very best,

1921
01:23:30.167 --> 01:23:32.268
but I must go.

1922
01:23:32.303 --> 01:23:34.137
Just stay
a little while longer.

1923
01:23:34.171 --> 01:23:36.473
No, no more time.
Goodbye.

1924
01:23:38.242 --> 01:23:40.276
We can talk about
something else, I swear.

1925
01:23:40.311 --> 01:23:42.245
I don't want to talk
about something else.

1926
01:23:42.279 --> 01:23:44.380
Don't go out there, Professor!
You know what's out there!

1927
01:23:44.415 --> 01:23:47.317
Oh yes.
Indeed I do.

1928
01:23:47.351 --> 01:23:50.286
I know what's out there
and I know who is out there.

1929
01:23:50.321 --> 01:23:52.288
I rush to nuzzle
his bony cheek.

1930
01:23:52.323 --> 01:23:55.558
No doubt he will be surprised
to find himself so cherished.

1931
01:23:55.593 --> 01:23:57.227
And as I cling to his neck

1932
01:23:57.261 --> 01:23:59.362
I will whisper in that
dry and ancient ear,

1933
01:23:59.396 --> 01:24:01.498
"Here I am.
Here I am."

1934
01:24:01.532 --> 01:24:03.600
- Now open the door!
- Don't do this.

1935
01:24:03.634 --> 01:24:06.269
You're a kind man.
I've heard you out

1936
01:24:06.303 --> 01:24:08.404
and you've heard me.
There's no more to say.

1937
01:24:08.439 --> 01:24:11.841
Your God must once have stood

1938
01:24:11.876 --> 01:24:13.977
at a dawn of infinite
possibilities,

1939
01:24:14.011 --> 01:24:16.746
and that is what
he's made of it.

1940
01:24:16.781 --> 01:24:19.849
You tell me that I want
God's love. I don't.

1941
01:24:19.884 --> 01:24:23.019
Perhaps I want forgiveness,
but there is no one to ask it of.

1942
01:24:23.053 --> 01:24:25.822
And there's no going back.
There's no setting things right.

1943
01:24:25.856 --> 01:24:28.224
There's only the hope
of nothingness.

1944
01:24:28.259 --> 01:24:30.460
And I cling to that hope.

1945
01:24:30.494 --> 01:24:32.729
- Now open the door.
- Don't do this.

1946
01:24:32.763 --> 01:24:35.331
Please open the door.

1947
01:25:13.571 --> 01:25:15.905
Thank you.
Goodbye.

1948
01:25:20.711 --> 01:25:22.846
Professor,

1949
01:25:22.880 --> 01:25:27.483
I know you ain't mean
them words.

1950
01:25:27.518 --> 01:25:29.886
I'm gonna be there
in the morning.

1951
01:25:31.355 --> 01:25:33.323
I'm gonna be there, you hear?

1952
01:25:35.159 --> 01:25:37.260
I'm gonna be there!

1953
01:25:39.730 --> 01:25:41.965
I'm gonna be there.

1954
01:25:47.037 --> 01:25:49.906
You know he didn't mean
them words.

1955
01:25:49.940 --> 01:25:52.575
You know he didn't.

1956
01:25:52.610 --> 01:25:55.545
You know he didn't.

1957
01:25:59.984 --> 01:26:03.753
I don't understand why
you sent me down there.

1958
01:26:03.787 --> 01:26:05.688
I don't understand.

1959
01:26:05.723 --> 01:26:09.192
If you wanted me to help him, then how
come you didn't give me the words?

1960
01:26:09.226 --> 01:26:12.428
You give them to him.
What about me?

1961
01:26:21.071 --> 01:26:25.074
That's all right.
That's all right.

1962
01:26:25.109 --> 01:26:28.111
If you don't never speak again,

1963
01:26:28.145 --> 01:26:32.015
you know I'll keep your word.

1964
01:26:32.049 --> 01:26:35.818
You know I will.
You know I'm good for it.

1965
01:26:39.256 --> 01:26:41.624
Is that okay?

1966
01:26:47.665 --> 01:26:49.799
Is that okay?
